R.facts('ch06-st1', F(
    terms=[('anarchy', 'in international relations, the absence of a central authority above sovereign states, not necessarily chaos'),
           ('security dilemma', 'a situation in which measures one state takes to increase its security make others feel less secure, provoking counter-measures that leave all less secure'),
           ('balance of power', 'a distribution of capabilities in which no state can dominate, maintained by internal arming or external alliances'),
           ('self-help', 'the realist idea that in an anarchic system states can rely only on themselves for survival'),
           ('offensive realism', 'John Mearsheimer\'s theory that the anarchic system pushes great powers to maximise relative power and seek regional hegemony'),
           ('defensive realism', 'the theory, associated with Kenneth Waltz, that states seek an appropriate amount of power to ensure security rather than maximise it')],
    facts=[('Which of the following realists wrote Politics Among Nations (1948)?', 'Hans Morgenthau', ['Kenneth Waltz', 'Robert Keohane', 'Alexander Wendt'], 'Morgenthau\'s classical realism rooted politics in human nature.'),
           ('Which of the following ancient historians recorded the Melian Dialogue, often cited by realists?', 'Thucydides', ['Herodotus', 'Plato', 'Cicero'], 'In the History of the Peloponnesian War, the Athenians say "the strong do what they can and the weak suffer what they must".'),
           ('Which of the following works set out neorealism (structural realism)?', 'Theory of International Politics by Kenneth Waltz (1979)', ['Perpetual Peace by Immanuel Kant (1795)', 'Power and Interdependence by Keohane and Nye (1977)', 'Leviathan by Thomas Hobbes (1651)'], 'Waltz explained state behaviour by the structure of the system rather than human nature.'),
           ('Which of the following scholars is associated with the "tragedy of great power politics" and offensive realism?', 'John Mearsheimer', ['Joseph Nye', 'Michael Doyle', 'Cynthia Enloe'], 'Mearsheimer\'s 2001 book argued great powers seek hegemony.')],
    why=[('Explain why realists believe states cannot fully trust one another.', 'Under anarchy there is no authority to enforce agreements, and states cannot know others\' present or future intentions, so they must prepare for the worst and rely on self-help.'),
         ('Explain why neorealists focus on the structure of the international system rather than on leaders.', 'Waltz argued that anarchy and the distribution of capabilities shape all states\' behaviour in similar ways regardless of their leaders or regime type, so structure explains recurring patterns such as balancing.'),
         ('Explain why the security dilemma can lead to arms races.', 'When one state builds weapons for defence, others cannot be sure it is not offensive and respond by arming too; each side\'s defensive measures appear threatening, producing escalation.')],
    contrast=[('classical realism', 'neorealism', 'Classical realism (Morgenthau) explains power politics through human nature and the desire for power; neorealism (Waltz) explains it through the anarchic structure of the international system.'),
              ('offensive realism', 'defensive realism', 'Offensive realism (Mearsheimer) says states maximise power to become hegemons; defensive realism (Waltz) says states seek enough power for security and that excessive expansion provokes balancing.')],
    misc=[('Realists believe that international anarchy means constant war.', 'Anarchy means no world government; realists accept that peace can exist through balance of power and deterrence, though conflict is always possible.'),
          ('Realists think international institutions are completely irrelevant.', 'Realists see institutions as reflecting the interests of powerful states (Mearsheimer\'s "false promise"), not as irrelevant – great powers use them as tools.')],
    cases=[('NATO enlargement and Russia\'s war in Ukraine', 'Mearsheimer has argued that NATO\'s eastward enlargement threatened Russia and helped provoke its actions in Ukraine; critics reply that this denies Ukraine\'s agency and Russia\'s imperial aims.', 'realist explanations of conflict', 'the case applies balance-of-power and security-dilemma logic, showing both the explanatory power and controversy of realism'),
           ('the AUKUS pact (2021)', 'Australia, the UK and the US agreed a security partnership under which Australia will acquire nuclear-powered submarines, widely seen as a response to China\'s naval build-up.', 'balancing behaviour', 'states facing a rising power formed an alliance and armed themselves, as realists predict'),
           ('the India–Pakistan nuclear rivalry', 'Both states tested nuclear weapons in 1998 and have since developed delivery systems; crises such as 2019 Balakot and the May 2025 clashes ended without nuclear use.', 'the security dilemma and deterrence', 'each side\'s weapons increased the other\'s insecurity, yet mutual deterrence has so far prevented full-scale war'),
           ('the South China Sea disputes', 'China has built artificial islands with military facilities, while the Philippines, Vietnam and others contest its claims; a 2016 arbitral tribunal rejected China\'s "nine-dash line", which China ignored.', 'power over law in an anarchic system', 'a powerful state disregarded an international ruling, consistent with realist expectations that power, not law, decides outcomes')],
    apply=[('Use the concept of the security dilemma to explain the reaction of Japan and South Korea to North Korea\'s missile tests.', 'North Korea\'s missiles (whatever their intent) make neighbours feel insecure, so Japan has increased defence spending and counter-strike capabilities and South Korea has strengthened alliances with the US, which North Korea in turn sees as threatening – a spiral.', 4),
           ('Explain how a neorealist would interpret the rise of China.', 'A shift in the distribution of capabilities towards China produces balancing by the US and its allies (Quad, AUKUS), and competition is driven by system structure rather than ideology or leaders.', 3)],
    sources=[('"The Athenians told the Melians: you know as well as we do that right, as the world goes, is only in question between equals in power, while the strong do what they can and the weak suffer what they must."', 'Thucydides, History of the Peloponnesian War (5th century BCE), trans. Richard Crawley', 'With reference to the source, explain the realist view of power and justice.', 'Justice only applies between equals; between unequal powers, outcomes are determined by power – a core realist claim that morality is subordinate to power in international relations.'),
             ('"Great powers fear each other. They regard each other with suspicion, and they worry that war might be in the offing."', 'Paraphrase of John Mearsheimer, The Tragedy of Great Power Politics (2001)', 'Explain, with reference to the source, why offensive realists expect competition among great powers.', 'Uncertainty about intentions under anarchy produces fear; to survive, great powers seek to maximise relative power, leading to competition and potential war.')],
    evaluate=[('Realism provides the best explanation of contemporary international relations.', ['Russia–Ukraine war, US–China rivalry, rearmament', 'balancing (AUKUS, NATO expansion after 2022)'],
               ['cooperation through institutions (EU, WTO)', 'non-state actors and norms (ICC, climate)', 'cannot explain end of Cold War well'],
               'Realism explains much of great-power security behaviour, but liberal and constructivist insights are needed for cooperation, norms and change.'),
              ('The security dilemma makes conflict between great powers inevitable.', ['arms races (US–China naval)', 'uncertainty under anarchy'],
               ['arms control (New START until 2026)', 'confidence-building and institutions', 'Wendt: anarchy is what states make of it'],
               'The security dilemma increases risks, but diplomacy and institutions can mitigate it, so conflict is not inevitable.')],
    extended=[('Analyse a contemporary conflict using realist concepts and evaluate the strengths and weaknesses of this explanation.', 'Apply anarchy, security dilemma, balance of power and national interest to e.g. Russia–Ukraine, Israel–Iran, or the South China Sea; then evaluate omissions (identity, domestic politics, international law).', 8, 'long')],
))

R.facts('ch06-st2', F(
    terms=[('liberalism', 'a perspective that emphasises individual rights, democracy, free trade and international institutions as ways to achieve cooperation and peace'),
           ('democratic peace theory', 'the claim that established liberal democracies rarely, if ever, go to war with one another'),
           ('neoliberal institutionalism', 'the view (Keohane) that international institutions help self-interested states cooperate by reducing transaction costs, providing information and enabling reciprocity'),
           ('absolute gains', 'the total benefits an actor receives from cooperation, regardless of how much others gain'),
           ('relative gains', 'the benefits an actor receives compared with those of others – the focus of realists'),
           ('liberal international order', 'the post-1945 system of open trade, multilateral institutions and democratic norms led largely by the US')],
    facts=[('Which of the following philosophers wrote Perpetual Peace (1795), an inspiration for democratic peace theory?', 'Immanuel Kant', ['Thomas Hobbes', 'Niccolò Machiavelli', 'Karl Marx'], 'Kant argued republics, commerce and a federation of free states could create lasting peace.'),
           ('Which of the following scholars wrote After Hegemony (1984), arguing institutions can sustain cooperation without a hegemon?', 'Robert Keohane', ['Kenneth Waltz', 'Hans Morgenthau', 'Edward Said'], 'Keohane is the key figure of neoliberal institutionalism.'),
           ('Which of the following US presidents proposed the League of Nations in his Fourteen Points (1918)?', 'Woodrow Wilson', ['Franklin D. Roosevelt', 'Theodore Roosevelt', 'Harry Truman'], 'Wilsonian idealism is a key strand of liberal thought.'),
           ('Which of the following argued in 1989 that liberal democracy might represent the "end of history"?', 'Francis Fukuyama', ['Samuel Huntington', 'Joseph Nye', 'John Rawls'], 'Fukuyama\'s essay became a 1992 book.')],
    why=[('Explain why liberals believe democracies rarely fight each other.', 'Normative explanation: shared norms of compromise and respect for rights; institutional explanation: leaders are accountable to voters, legislatures and free media, which raise the costs of war; transparency reduces miscalculation.'),
         ('Explain why neoliberal institutionalists think cooperation is possible under anarchy.', 'Institutions create repeated interactions ("shadow of the future"), share information, monitor compliance and lower the costs of agreements, so states focused on absolute gains can cooperate.'),
         ('Explain why liberals support free trade as a path to peace.', 'Trade creates mutual benefits and interdependence; war would destroy these gains, and commercial ties build cross-border networks and understanding (commercial liberalism, from Kant and Cobden to Keohane and Nye).')],
    contrast=[('absolute gains', 'relative gains', 'Absolute gains focus on how much a state gains overall (liberal view); relative gains focus on how much it gains compared with rivals (realist view), which can block cooperation even when all would gain.'),
              ('liberalism', 'realism', 'Liberalism sees cooperation, institutions, democracy and interdependence as able to reduce conflict and considers non-state actors important; realism sees states pursuing power and security under anarchy with limited prospects for lasting cooperation.')],
    misc=[('Democratic peace theory claims that democracies never go to war.', 'It claims democracies rarely fight each other; democracies frequently fight non-democracies (e.g. Iraq 2003).'),
          ('Liberals ignore power in international relations.', 'Liberals acknowledge power but argue it can be constrained by institutions, law and interdependence; Nye\'s soft power is a liberal contribution to understanding power.')],
    cases=[('the European Union', 'Former enemies France and Germany built economic and political integration after 1950 (ECSC, EEC, EU); war between members has become unthinkable, and the EU won the 2012 Nobel Peace Prize.', 'liberal institutionalism', 'institutions and interdependence transformed relations among historic rivals into stable cooperation'),
           ('the Montreal Protocol (1987)', 'States agreed to phase out ozone-depleting CFCs; it is the first UN treaty ratified by all UN members, and the ozone layer is recovering.', 'cooperation through international regimes', 'a treaty with scientific monitoring, financial support for developing states and trade measures solved a collective action problem'),
           ('the 2015 Iran nuclear deal (JCPOA)', 'Iran agreed to limit its nuclear programme in return for sanctions relief; the US withdrew in 2018, and Iran expanded enrichment afterwards.', 'the strengths and limits of liberal institutionalism', 'negotiation and verification produced cooperation, but its collapse after US withdrawal showed the fragility of institutions when powerful states defect'),
           ('the Association of Southeast Asian Nations\' economic integration', 'ASEAN created a free trade area (AFTA) and helped build the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership, which entered into force in 2022.', 'commercial liberalism', 'growing trade ties among diverse regimes have supported decades without inter-state war among members')],
    apply=[('Explain how a liberal would explain the absence of war between the United States and Canada.', 'Both are established democracies with shared norms (democratic peace), deep economic interdependence (USMCA trade) and dense institutions (NATO, NORAD), raising the costs of conflict and building trust.', 3),
           ('Two states can sign a trade deal from which both gain, but one gains much more. Explain how a liberal and a realist would each evaluate the deal.', 'Liberal: both make absolute gains, so the deal is rational and builds cooperation. Realist: the state gaining less worries about the relative gain of its rival, which may later be turned into power, so it may reject the deal.', 4)],
    sources=[('"The absence of war between liberal democracies comes as close as anything we have to an empirical law in international relations."', 'Paraphrase of Jack Levy, political scientist, 1988', 'With reference to the source, explain democratic peace theory.', 'Liberal democracies rarely fight each other because of shared norms and institutional constraints; the source claims this pattern is unusually robust.'),
             ('"Institutions do not make states good. They make it easier for self-interested states to keep their promises."', 'Lecture on neoliberal institutionalism, 2019 (adapted)', 'Explain, with reference to the source, how international institutions promote cooperation.', 'Institutions provide information, monitoring and repeated interaction, reducing cheating and transaction costs, so even self-interested states can cooperate – they change incentives rather than values.')],
    evaluate=[('Democratic peace theory is the most convincing liberal argument about international relations.', ['strong statistical pattern since 1945', 'clear causal mechanisms'],
               ['definitional problems (what counts as democracy?)', 'Cold War alliances may explain peace', 'institutional and commercial liberalism equally strong'],
               'Democratic peace is among the strongest empirical findings, but its explanation is debated and other liberal arguments are needed to explain cooperation beyond democracies.'),
              ('The liberal international order is collapsing.', ['US tariffs and withdrawals', 'Russia\'s invasion of Ukraine', 'WTO paralysis', 'democratic backsliding'],
               ['NATO and EU strengthened after 2022', 'trade remains high', 'new regional agreements', 'UN still central'],
               'The order is weakening and fragmenting, but key institutions persist and adapt, so "collapse" overstates the trend.')],
    extended=[('Compare how realism and liberalism explain the creation and survival of NATO after the Cold War.', 'Realism: alliance against a threat; expected to dissolve after 1991 (Waltz) but survived due to US power and renewed Russian threat. Liberalism: institution with shared values and sunk costs, adapting to new tasks; enlargement to Finland (2023) and Sweden (2024). Judge which explains better.', 8, 'long')],
))

R.facts('ch06-st3', F(
    terms=[('constructivism', 'a perspective arguing that international relations are shaped by shared ideas, identities and norms, not only material power'),
           ('norm', 'a shared standard of appropriate behaviour for actors with a given identity'),
           ('norm life cycle', 'Finnemore and Sikkink\'s model in which norms emerge through norm entrepreneurs, cascade through states and are finally internalised'),
           ('norm entrepreneur', 'an actor who promotes a new norm by framing an issue and persuading others, such as an NGO or individual campaigner'),
           ('identity', 'an actor\'s understanding of who it is and its relationship to others, which shapes its interests'),
           ('securitization', 'the process by which an issue is presented as an existential threat requiring extraordinary measures (Copenhagen School)')],
    facts=[('Which of the following scholars wrote "Anarchy is what states make of it" (1992)?', 'Alexander Wendt', ['Kenneth Waltz', 'Robert Keohane', 'Susan Strange'], 'Wendt argued that anarchy has no fixed logic; its meaning depends on shared ideas.'),
           ('Which of the following scholars developed the "norm life cycle" model in 1998?', 'Martha Finnemore and Kathryn Sikkink', ['Keohane and Nye', 'Chenoweth and Stephan', 'Levitsky and Way'], 'Their article appeared in International Organization.'),
           ('Which of the following is associated with the concept of securitization?', 'The Copenhagen School (Barry Buzan and Ole Wæver)', ['The Frankfurt School', 'The English School of Hedley Bull', 'The Chicago School of economics'], 'Securitization explains how issues are constructed as security threats.'),
           ('Which of the following examples best illustrates the constructivist argument that identity shapes threat perception?', 'The UK does not feel threatened by France\'s nuclear weapons but would by North Korea\'s', ['States always balance against the most powerful state', 'Trade between states always reduces conflict', 'Economic classes determine foreign policy'], 'Wendt noted that 500 British nuclear weapons are less threatening to the US than five North Korean ones.')],
    why=[('Explain why constructivists argue that state interests are not fixed.', 'Interests derive from identities formed through interaction and shared norms; as identities change (e.g. Germany\'s post-war identity as a "civilian power"), so do interests.'),
         ('Explain why norm entrepreneurs are important in constructivist theory.', 'They frame issues in new ways, mobilise support and persuade states to adopt new standards; e.g. ICBL framed landmines as a humanitarian issue, leading to the Ottawa Treaty.'),
         ('Explain why constructivism helps explain the end of the Cold War.', 'Material power did not suddenly change; Gorbachev\'s "new thinking" redefined Soviet identity and interests, ending ideological confrontation – a change in ideas that realism struggled to predict.')],
    contrast=[('constructivism', 'realism', 'Constructivism argues that anarchy and interests are socially constructed through ideas and identities and can change; realism treats anarchy and the pursuit of power as fixed features driving state behaviour.'),
              ('a norm', 'a law', 'A norm is a shared expectation of appropriate behaviour, which may be informal; a law is a formal, codified rule with legal enforcement – norms can become laws (e.g. the anti-landmine norm in the Ottawa Treaty).')],
    misc=[('Constructivists think material power is irrelevant.', 'Constructivists accept that material factors matter but argue their meaning depends on ideas – weapons are threatening only in the context of hostile identities.'),
          ('Once a norm exists it can never be weakened.', 'Norms can be contested and eroded; e.g. the norm against territorial conquest was challenged by Russia\'s annexation of Crimea and the torture taboo by US "enhanced interrogation" after 2001.')],
    cases=[('the Ottawa Treaty banning landmines (1997)', 'Campaigners framed anti-personnel landmines as indiscriminate weapons that harm civilians long after war; more than 160 states joined, though the US, China and Russia did not. In 2025 several European states bordering Russia announced withdrawal.', 'the norm life cycle', 'NGO norm entrepreneurs created a cascade of state adoption, but recent withdrawals show norms can be reversed under security pressure'),
           ('the nuclear taboo', 'Nuclear weapons have not been used in war since 1945, which scholars such as Nina Tannenwald attribute partly to a normative stigma against their use.', 'norms shaping state behaviour', 'states with nuclear weapons have refrained from using them even in wars they lost, suggesting a norm, not only deterrence'),
           ('Germany\'s Zeitenwende (2022)', 'After Russia\'s invasion of Ukraine, Chancellor Scholz announced a €100 billion fund for the armed forces, marking a shift from Germany\'s post-war identity of military restraint.', 'changing identity and interests', 'a shock changed how Germany understood its role, leading to new interests in defence'),
           ('the securitization of migration in Europe', 'Some European governments have framed migration as a threat to security and identity, justifying border fences and offshore processing arrangements.', 'securitization', 'political speech turned a social issue into a security threat, legitimising extraordinary measures')],
    apply=[('Using the norm life cycle, explain the spread of the idea that states should ban child soldiers.', 'Emergence: NGOs (Coalition to Stop the Use of Child Soldiers) and UN reports framed the issue; cascade: the 2000 Optional Protocol to the CRC and UNSC Res. 1612 (2005) – many states joined; internalisation: national laws and military recruitment rules accept 18 as the norm. 1 mark per stage + example.', 4),
           ('Explain how a constructivist would explain why the US and the UK are close allies despite occasional disagreements.', 'Shared identity (language, democracy, history), the "special relationship" narrative and institutional habits (Five Eyes, NATO) shape interests, so disputes are framed within a friendly relationship rather than as threats.', 3)],
    sources=[('"Five hundred British nuclear weapons are less threatening to the United States than five North Korean nuclear weapons, because the British are friends of the United States and the North Koreans are not."', 'Paraphrase of Alexander Wendt, Social Theory of International Politics (1999)', 'With reference to the source, explain the constructivist view of threat.', 'Threat depends on shared ideas and identities (friend or enemy), not on material capabilities alone; the meaning of weapons is socially constructed.'),
             ('"Twenty years ago no one talked about \'loss and damage\'. Today it is the language of every climate summit."', 'Climate negotiator from a Pacific Island state, 2023 (adapted)', 'Explain, with reference to the source, how new norms emerge in global politics.', 'Vulnerable states and NGOs acted as norm entrepreneurs, framing climate impacts as a justice issue; the idea cascaded until a Loss and Damage Fund was agreed at COP27 (2022).')],
    evaluate=[('Constructivism explains change in international politics better than realism.', ['end of the Cold War', 'spread of human rights and anti-landmine norms', 'Germany\'s identity change'],
               ['realists explain recurring power politics', 'norms often follow power (US hegemony)', 'constructivism weak at prediction'],
               'Constructivism is stronger at explaining change and the role of ideas, while realism explains continuity; both are partial.'),
              ('International norms significantly constrain the behaviour of powerful states.', ['nuclear taboo', 'states justify actions in legal terms', 'reputational costs'],
               ['Russia\'s annexations, US torture after 2001', 'withdrawals from Ottawa Treaty', 'selective enforcement'],
               'Norms shape how powerful states justify and sometimes limit their actions, but rarely stop them when core interests are at stake.')],
    extended=[('Analyse how a constructivist would explain one contemporary global political issue, and evaluate this explanation.', 'Choose e.g. securitization of migration, climate justice norms, or the nuclear taboo; apply identity, norms, norm entrepreneurs and discourse; evaluate against realist/liberal alternatives.', 8, 'long')],
))

R.facts('ch06-st4', F(
    terms=[('Marxism', 'a perspective that analyses politics in terms of class struggle and the capitalist system, seeing states as serving dominant economic interests'),
           ('feminism (in global politics)', 'a set of perspectives that examine how gender shapes and is shaped by global politics, asking "where are the women?"'),
           ('postcolonialism', 'a perspective that examines the lasting effects of colonialism on power, knowledge and identity in the contemporary world'),
           ('Orientalism', 'Edward Said\'s concept of Western representations of "the East" as exotic, backward and inferior, which justified colonial domination'),
           ('patriarchy', 'a social system in which men hold primary power and dominate political, economic and social institutions'),
           ('imperialism', 'the extension of a state\'s power over other territories and peoples, which Lenin described as the "highest stage of capitalism"')],
    facts=[('Which of the following scholars wrote Bananas, Beaches and Bases (1989), asking "where are the women?" in international politics?', 'Cynthia Enloe', ['J. Ann Tickner', 'Judith Butler', 'Hannah Arendt'], 'Enloe showed how women\'s labour sustains global politics.'),
           ('Which of the following wrote Orientalism (1978)?', 'Edward Said', ['Frantz Fanon', 'Gayatri Spivak', 'Homi Bhabha'], 'Said is a founder of postcolonial studies.'),
           ('Which of the following critical theorists wrote that "theory is always for someone and for some purpose"?', 'Robert Cox', ['Antonio Gramsci', 'Karl Marx', 'Immanuel Wallerstein'], 'Cox\'s 1981 article distinguished problem-solving from critical theory.'),
           ('Which of the following UN Security Council resolutions launched the Women, Peace and Security agenda in 2000?', 'Resolution 1325', ['Resolution 1973', 'Resolution 242', 'Resolution 2758'], 'It calls for women\'s participation in peace processes and protection from sexual violence.')],
    why=[('Explain why Marxists see international institutions such as the IMF as serving capitalist interests.', 'They argue the IMF and World Bank promote open markets, privatisation and debt repayment that benefit capital in the core, while imposing austerity on workers in the periphery – reproducing global class inequalities.'),
         ('Explain why feminists argue that traditional theories of international relations are incomplete.', 'Realism and liberalism focus on states, war and diplomacy dominated by men, ignoring women\'s experiences (sexual violence in war, care work, migration, peace activism) and how gendered ideas of masculinity shape security policy.'),
         ('Explain why postcolonial scholars criticise the idea of "universal" values.', 'They argue that ideas presented as universal often originate in Europe and were used to justify colonial rule ("civilising mission"); today they may still be applied selectively by former colonial powers.')],
    contrast=[('liberal feminism', 'critical feminism', 'Liberal feminism seeks equal participation of women within existing institutions (more women leaders, peace negotiators); critical/post-structural feminism questions the gendered structures and assumptions of those institutions themselves.'),
              ('Marxism', 'postcolonialism', 'Marxism explains global inequality mainly through class and the capitalist mode of production; postcolonialism emphasises the legacies of colonial domination, race and culture, and how knowledge and representation reproduce power.')],
    misc=[('Feminist approaches are only interested in the number of women in government.', 'Feminist approaches also analyse gendered power structures, masculinities, sexual violence, care economies and how security is defined – not just representation.'),
          ('Marxism is irrelevant after the end of the Cold War.', 'Marxist and neo-Marxist analysis remains influential in explaining global inequality, labour exploitation, debt and the power of capital (e.g. world-systems theory, critiques of austerity).')],
    cases=[('women in the Colombian peace process', 'The 2016 agreement between the government and the FARC included a gender sub-commission and provisions on women and LGBT victims, after pressure from women\'s organizations.', 'feminist perspectives on peace', 'including women and gender issues broadened the meaning of security and justice in the peace agreement'),
           ('the Rhodes Must Fall movement', 'Students at the University of Cape Town campaigned in 2015 for removal of a statue of Cecil Rhodes; it was removed and the movement spread to Oxford.', 'postcolonial critiques of power and knowledge', 'the movement challenged the symbolic legacies of colonialism and demanded "decolonised" curricula'),
           ('sovereign debt in Africa', 'In 2022 several African states spent more on debt servicing than on health; creditors include private bondholders, China and multilateral lenders.', 'Marxist and dependency critiques', 'debt flows transfer resources from periphery to core creditors, which critical theorists see as structural exploitation'),
           ('the Women, Peace and Security agenda', 'UNSC Resolution 1325 (2000) and later resolutions called for women\'s participation in peace processes, but women remain a small minority of negotiators.', 'gender and global governance', 'the agenda institutionalised a feminist critique, though implementation gaps show persistent gendered power')],
    apply=[('Explain how a feminist analysis of a war such as the conflict in Sudan might differ from a realist analysis.', 'Realist: focus on armed actors (SAF vs RSF), territory and outside powers\' interests. Feminist: focus on sexual violence as a weapon, displacement and care burdens of women, women\'s grassroots responders, and militarised masculinity.', 4),
           ('Identify how each perspective would explain global inequality: (a) Marxism, (b) postcolonialism, (c) feminism.', '(a) class exploitation by global capital; (b) colonial legacies and racialised hierarchies; (c) gendered division of labour and undervalued care work. 1 mark each.', 3)],
    sources=[('"If we made women\'s experiences central, we would have to rethink what we mean by security – not only borders and missiles, but safety from violence at home, food and clean water."', 'Paraphrase of J. Ann Tickner, Gender in International Relations (1992)', 'With reference to the source, explain how feminism redefines security.', 'Feminism broadens security from state/military security to human security, including protection from domestic violence and access to basic needs – drawing on women\'s experiences.'),
             ('"They drew borders across our continent with rulers and pencils in Berlin. We are still living inside their maps."', 'Speech by an African politician, 2022 (adapted)', 'Explain, with reference to the source, a postcolonial perspective on African politics.', 'Colonial powers at the 1884–85 Berlin Conference drew borders without regard to local societies; these borders persist and shape conflicts and identities, showing ongoing colonial legacies.')],
    evaluate=[('Critical perspectives are more useful than mainstream theories for understanding inequality in global politics.', ['Marxism explains structural economic inequality', 'feminism reveals gendered harms', 'postcolonialism exposes colonial legacies'],
               ['liberalism explains poverty reduction through trade', 'critical theories offer limited policy solutions', 'realism explains power hierarchies'],
               'Critical perspectives illuminate the structural roots of inequality that mainstream theories miss, but are less developed in offering practical solutions.'),
              ('Feminist perspectives have changed how global institutions understand security.', ['UNSCR 1325 and WPS agenda', 'sexual violence recognised as war crime (ICTY, ICTR)', 'feminist foreign policies (Sweden 2014–2022)'],
               ['women still a small minority of negotiators', 'Sweden dropped its feminist foreign policy in 2022', 'militarised security dominates budgets'],
               'Feminist ideas have entered institutional language and law, but practice lags significantly behind.')],
    extended=[('Using one critical perspective, analyse a global political issue you have studied and evaluate what this perspective reveals that realism does not.', 'E.g. postcolonial analysis of the Rohingya crisis or Western Sahara; Marxist analysis of debt or labour in supply chains; feminist analysis of conflict-related sexual violence. Compare explicitly with realism.', 8, 'long')],
))

