# =============================================================================================
# ch07 Rights and Justice – nature and evolution of human rights
# =============================================================================================
R.facts('ch07-st1', F(
    terms=[('human rights', 'basic rights and freedoms to which all people are entitled simply because they are human, regardless of nationality, sex, ethnicity or other status'),
           ('natural rights', 'rights believed to exist independently of law or government, derived from human nature or reason, such as Locke\'s life, liberty and property'),
           ('legal rights', 'rights recognised and protected by a specific legal system, such as rights in a national constitution or a treaty'),
           ('moral rights', 'rights grounded in ethical principles that people ought to have, whether or not the law recognises them'),
           ('justice', 'the fair treatment of people and the fair distribution of benefits, burdens and rights in society'),
           ('inalienable', 'describing rights that cannot be given up, sold or taken away')],
    facts=[('Which of the following thinkers argued that people have natural rights to "life, liberty and estate"?', 'John Locke', ['Thomas Hobbes', 'Jeremy Bentham', 'Karl Marx'], 'Locke set this out in the Second Treatise of Government (1689).'),
           ('Which of the following philosophers dismissed natural rights as "nonsense upon stilts"?', 'Jeremy Bentham', ['John Locke', 'Thomas Paine', 'Immanuel Kant'], 'Bentham argued that only legal rights created by law are real.'),
           ('Which of the following documents of 1789 proclaimed that "men are born and remain free and equal in rights"?', 'The French Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen', ['The US Bill of Rights', 'The Magna Carta', 'The English Bill of Rights'], 'Article 1 of the French Declaration.'),
           ('Which of the following is the best example of a legal right that is not universally accepted as a moral right?', 'A right to carry firearms under the US Second Amendment', ['The right not to be tortured', 'The right to life', 'The right not to be enslaved'], 'The right to bear arms is legally protected in the US but widely contested as a moral right elsewhere.')],
    why=[('Explain why natural rights are said to exist even where laws do not recognise them.', 'Natural rights theorists argue rights derive from human nature or reason, not from the state; they therefore exist prior to government and can be used to criticise unjust laws (e.g. against slavery or apartheid).'),
         ('Explain why legal rights can differ between countries.', 'Legal rights depend on each state\'s constitution, legislation and treaty commitments; e.g. same-sex marriage is a legal right in some states but illegal in others.'),
         ('Explain why rights and justice are closely connected.', 'Rights set out what individuals are owed; justice concerns whether a society gives people what they are owed and distributes benefits and burdens fairly, so violations of rights are injustices.')],
    contrast=[('natural rights', 'legal rights', 'Natural rights are held to exist by virtue of being human, independent of law; legal rights exist because a legal system recognises and enforces them and can be changed by legislation.'),
              ('negative rights', 'positive rights', 'Negative rights require others (especially the state) to refrain from interfering, e.g. freedom of speech; positive rights require action or provision, e.g. a right to education or health care.')],
    misc=[('Human rights only exist if a country\'s government has written them into law.', 'This confuses legal rights with human rights; human rights are claimed to belong to everyone, and international law (UDHR, treaties) recognises them even where national law does not.'),
          ('Rights and justice mean exactly the same thing.', 'Rights are entitlements of individuals or groups; justice is a broader principle of fairness about how a society treats and distributes among its members.')],
    cases=[('the abolition of slavery', 'Abolitionists such as Frederick Douglass appealed to natural and moral rights to oppose slavery when it was still legal in the US until 1865.', 'the difference between moral and legal rights', 'campaigners used moral rights claims to challenge legal systems that denied rights, eventually changing the law'),
           ('the Universal Declaration of Human Rights in apartheid South Africa', 'The apartheid state denied black South Africans voting and movement rights; the ANC\'s 1955 Freedom Charter claimed equal rights for all.', 'rights as a basis for justice claims', 'movements appealed to universal rights to expose the injustice of a legal order and build international pressure'),
           ('the 2017 Indian Supreme Court privacy ruling (Puttaswamy)', 'A nine-judge bench unanimously held that privacy is a fundamental right under the Indian Constitution.', 'how legal rights are recognised', 'the court turned a moral claim to privacy into an enforceable constitutional right')],
    apply=[('Classify each right as natural, legal or moral (choose the best fit) and justify: (a) a right to vote at 16 in Austria, (b) the right not to be tortured, (c) a claim that future generations have a right to a liveable climate.', '(a) legal – created by Austrian law (2007); (b) widely seen as natural/human right, also legal under the Convention against Torture; (c) moral – argued ethically, only partly recognised in law (e.g. some court cases). 1 mark each.', 3),
           ('A protester says: "The law says I cannot criticise the king, but I have a right to speak freely." Identify the two types of rights in conflict and explain.', 'Legal restriction (lèse-majesté law, e.g. in Thailand) vs natural/moral/human right to freedom of expression (UDHR Art. 19); the protester appeals to a right above national law.', 3)],
    sources=[('"Rights are not gifts from governments. Governments are created to secure them, and when they fail, the people may change them."', 'Paraphrase of the US Declaration of Independence (1776)', 'With reference to the source, explain the idea of natural rights.', 'Rights exist before government and are not granted by it; government\'s legitimacy depends on protecting them – Locke\'s natural rights theory.'),
             ('"A right without a remedy is no right at all. Unless a court can enforce it, a right is only a hope."', 'Legal commentary, 2018 (adapted)', 'Identify the view of rights in the source and explain one criticism of it.', 'A legal positivist view – rights only exist meaningfully when enforceable by law. Criticism: moral and human rights can exist and motivate change before courts recognise them (e.g. abolition, civil rights).')],
    evaluate=[('Human rights are best understood as legal rather than natural rights.', ['legal rights are enforceable and clear', 'Bentham: natural rights are nonsense'],
               ['natural rights justify challenging unjust laws', 'UDHR grounds rights in human dignity'],
               'Legal recognition makes rights effective, but natural and moral foundations explain why they should be recognised at all.'),
              ('Rights are meaningless without effective enforcement.', ['violations go unpunished (Syria, Myanmar)', 'rights depend on courts'],
               ['rights empower movements and set standards', 'naming and shaming changes behaviour'],
               'Enforcement makes rights effective, but rights also have normative and mobilising power even when weakly enforced.')],
    extended=[('Discuss the extent to which the concept of natural rights remains useful in contemporary global politics.', 'Discuss foundations (Locke, UDHR "inherent dignity"), criticisms (Bentham, cultural relativism), and contemporary use (civil disobedience, climate litigation, refugee claims). Reach a judgement.', 8, 'long')],
))

R.facts('ch07-st2', F(
    terms=[('first-generation rights', 'civil and political rights, such as freedom of speech, a fair trial and the right to vote, mainly requiring the state not to interfere'),
           ('second-generation rights', 'economic, social and cultural rights, such as the rights to work, education, health and housing, requiring state provision'),
           ('third-generation rights', 'collective or solidarity rights, such as the rights to development, peace and a healthy environment'),
           ('collective rights', 'rights held by groups rather than individuals, such as the right of Indigenous peoples to self-determination'),
           ('progressive realisation', 'the principle that states must take steps, to the maximum of available resources, to achieve economic, social and cultural rights over time (ICESCR Art. 2)'),
           ('indivisibility of rights', 'the principle that all human rights are equally important and interdependent, affirmed in the 1993 Vienna Declaration')],
    facts=[('Which of the following jurists proposed the "three generations" of human rights in 1977?', 'Karel Vasak', ['René Cassin', 'Hersch Lauterpacht', 'Raphael Lemkin'], 'Vasak linked the generations to liberty, equality and fraternity.'),
           ('Which of the following UN General Assembly resolutions of July 2022 recognised the right to a clean, healthy and sustainable environment?', 'Resolution 76/300', ['Resolution 217A', 'Resolution 1325', 'Resolution 64/292'], 'Resolution 64/292 (2010) recognised the right to water and sanitation.'),
           ('Which of the following is a second-generation right?', 'The right to education', ['Freedom of assembly', 'The right to a fair trial', 'The right to development'], 'Education is an economic, social and cultural right (ICESCR Art. 13).'),
           ('Which of the following documents (1986) proclaimed the right to development?', 'The UN Declaration on the Right to Development', ['The Rome Statute', 'The Geneva Conventions', 'The Paris Agreement'], 'It is a key third-generation rights document.')],
    why=[('Explain why second-generation rights were historically associated with socialist states.', 'During the Cold War, the Soviet bloc emphasised economic and social rights (work, housing, health) as essential, while Western states emphasised civil and political rights; this is one reason the UDHR rights were split into two covenants in 1966.'),
         ('Explain why some critics argue that the "generations" model is misleading.', 'It implies a hierarchy and a sequence, but rights are indivisible and interdependent (Vienna 1993): e.g. the right to vote means little without education, and many socio-economic rights were demanded alongside civil rights.'),
         ('Explain why collective rights can conflict with individual rights.', 'A group\'s right to preserve its culture may justify practices that limit individuals\' choices – e.g. community control over land or customary rules about membership and gender roles.')],
    contrast=[('civil and political rights', 'economic, social and cultural rights', 'Civil and political rights protect freedom from state interference and participation (speech, trial, vote) and are immediately applicable; economic, social and cultural rights require provision (health, education, housing) and are realised progressively according to resources.'),
              ('individual rights', 'collective rights', 'Individual rights are held by each person (e.g. freedom of religion); collective rights are held by groups as such (e.g. a people\'s right to self-determination or Indigenous land rights).')],
    misc=[('Economic and social rights are not real human rights because they cost money.', 'All rights cost money – fair trials need courts and elections need administration; the ICESCR makes socio-economic rights legally binding on 170+ states parties, subject to progressive realisation.'),
          ('Third-generation rights are legally binding in the same way as first-generation rights.', 'Most third-generation rights are in declarations or emerging law and are less enforceable; though regional instruments such as the African Charter (1981) include peoples\' rights.')],
    cases=[('South Africa\'s Grootboom case (2000)', 'The Constitutional Court held that the state\'s housing programme was unreasonable because it made no provision for people in desperate need, enforcing the constitutional right to access to housing.', 'the justiciability of socio-economic rights', 'a court enforced a second-generation right, showing such rights can be legally meaningful'),
           ('Inter-American Court and Indigenous land rights (Awas Tingni v Nicaragua, 2001)', 'The court ruled that Nicaragua violated the Mayagna community\'s right to property by granting logging concessions on their land.', 'collective rights', 'a regional court recognised a group\'s communal right to land, extending property rights to collective form'),
           ('UNGA recognition of the right to a healthy environment (2022)', 'In July 2022 the General Assembly voted 161–0 with 8 abstentions to recognise the right to a clean, healthy and sustainable environment.', 'the emergence of third-generation rights', 'states recognised a new solidarity right, though it is not yet a binding treaty obligation'),
           ('the right to water and sanitation', 'The UN General Assembly recognised the right in 2010; Detroit\'s mass water shut-offs in 2014 were criticised by UN experts.', 'socio-economic rights in developed countries', 'it showed that second-generation rights are at stake in wealthy states too, not only in developing countries')],
    apply=[('Classify each as first-, second- or third-generation rights: (a) the right to join a trade union, (b) the right to vote, (c) the right of peoples to peace, (d) the right to health.', '(a) second (economic right, ICESCR Art. 8; also civil right of association – accept with reason); (b) first; (c) third; (d) second. 1 mark each.', 4),
           ('A government says it cannot afford universal primary health care. Explain how the principle of progressive realisation applies.', 'Under ICESCR Art. 2(1), the state must take deliberate steps to the maximum of available resources, avoid retrogression and prioritise minimum core obligations (essential primary care); lack of resources does not remove the obligation.', 4)],
    sources=[('"What use is the right to vote to a man who cannot read the ballot or a woman who is too hungry to queue?"', 'Statement by a development activist, 2015 (adapted)', 'With reference to the source, explain the idea that rights are indivisible.', 'Civil and political rights (voting) depend on socio-economic rights (education, food); without the latter, the former cannot be exercised, so rights are interdependent.'),
             ('"We do not ask for charity. We ask for the right to our land, our language and our way of life as a people."', 'Statement by an Indigenous leader at the UN Permanent Forum, 2019 (adapted)', 'Identify the type of rights claimed in the source and explain it.', 'Collective (third-generation / group) rights – rights held by a people to land, language and culture, recognised in UNDRIP (2007).')],
    evaluate=[('Civil and political rights are more important than economic and social rights.', ['protect against tyranny', 'enable people to demand other rights', 'immediately enforceable'],
               ['survival needs come first (Sen, capabilities)', 'indivisibility (Vienna 1993)', 'hunger undermines freedom'],
               'The two sets of rights are interdependent; prioritising one undermines the other, so ranking them is misleading.'),
              ('Third-generation rights weaken the concept of human rights.', ['vague and hard to enforce', 'unclear who holds the duty', 'dilute core rights'],
               ['address real global problems (climate, development)', 'reflect Global South priorities', 'build on solidarity'],
               'They broaden human rights to global challenges, but their weak enforceability means they complement rather than replace core rights.')],
    extended=[('Analyse how one contemporary issue involves rights from more than one generation.', 'E.g. climate change: right to life (first), health and food (second), healthy environment and development (third); Indigenous land conflicts; digital rights. Show interdependence and tensions.', 8, 'long')],
))

R.facts('ch07-st3', F(
    terms=[('Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR)', 'the declaration adopted by the UN General Assembly on 10 December 1948 that sets out 30 articles of fundamental rights for all people'),
           ('International Bill of Human Rights', 'the UDHR together with the ICCPR and ICESCR (1966) and their optional protocols'),
           ('Commission on Human Rights', 'the UN body (1946–2006) that drafted the UDHR, chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt, later replaced by the Human Rights Council'),
           ('Human Rights Day', '10 December, the anniversary of the adoption of the UDHR in 1948'),
           ('customary international law', 'binding rules derived from consistent state practice accepted as law; some UDHR provisions are argued to have this status')],
    facts=[('Which of the following chaired the UN Commission on Human Rights that drafted the UDHR?', 'Eleanor Roosevelt', ['René Cassin', 'Dag Hammarskjöld', 'Ralph Bunche'], 'Cassin, P.C. Chang, Charles Malik and John Humphrey were key drafters.'),
           ('Which of the following describes the General Assembly vote on the UDHR in 1948?', '48 in favour, 0 against and 8 abstentions', ['Unanimous with 193 votes', '35 in favour and 20 against', '56 in favour and 2 against'], 'The Soviet bloc, South Africa and Saudi Arabia abstained.'),
           ('Which of the following is in Article 1 of the UDHR?', '"All human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights."', ['"Everyone has the right to bear arms."', '"Sovereignty belongs to the nation alone."', '"The state shall provide employment to all citizens."'], 'Article 1 also says they are endowed with reason and conscience.'),
           ('Which of the following events most directly shaped the drafting of the UDHR?', 'The atrocities of the Second World War, including the Holocaust', ['The end of the Cold War', 'The Arab Spring', 'The founding of the European Union'], 'The preamble refers to "barbarous acts which have outraged the conscience of mankind".')],
    why=[('Explain why the UDHR was adopted as a declaration rather than a binding treaty.', 'Cold War divisions and states\' concerns about sovereignty made a binding treaty impossible in 1948; a declaration allowed agreement on principles, while binding covenants followed in 1966.'),
         ('Explain why some states abstained in the 1948 vote on the UDHR.', 'The Soviet bloc objected that it emphasised civil liberties over socio-economic rights and state sovereignty; South Africa objected to equality provisions that challenged apartheid; Saudi Arabia objected to freedom to change religion and equal marriage rights.'),
         ('Explain why the UDHR has become influential despite not being legally binding.', 'It has been referenced in constitutions of many newly independent states, inspired binding treaties (ICCPR, ICESCR, CEDAW), shaped regional systems and is widely cited as reflecting customary international law.')],
    contrast=[('the UDHR', 'the ICCPR', 'The UDHR (1948) is a non-binding General Assembly declaration covering all categories of rights; the ICCPR (1966, in force 1976) is a binding treaty for its states parties covering civil and political rights, monitored by the Human Rights Committee.'),
              ('a declaration', 'a convention', 'A declaration states principles and commitments but is not legally binding by itself; a convention (treaty) is legally binding on states that ratify it.')],
    misc=[('The UDHR was signed and ratified as a treaty by all UN member states in 1948.', 'It was adopted by a General Assembly vote (48–0–8) by the then 58 members (two absent); declarations are not ratified.'),
          ('The UDHR was written only by Western drafters.', 'The drafting committee included Peng Chun Chang (China), Charles Malik (Lebanon), Hansa Mehta (India) and others; Mehta is credited with changing "all men" to "all human beings" in Article 1.')],
    cases=[('Hansa Mehta and the language of Article 1', 'The Indian delegate Hansa Mehta argued that "all men are born free and equal" should become "all human beings", making the text gender-inclusive.', 'non-Western contributions to the UDHR', 'a delegate from a newly independent Asian state shaped the universal language of the declaration'),
           ('the UDHR and decolonisation', 'Newly independent states such as Ghana and India drew on UDHR principles; the 1960 Declaration on Granting Independence to Colonial Countries (Res. 1514) linked self-determination to human rights.', 'the evolution of human rights after 1948', 'anti-colonial movements used human rights language to challenge empire, extending universal rights to colonised peoples'),
           ('the Nuremberg trials (1945–1946)', 'Leading Nazi officials were tried for crimes against peace, war crimes and crimes against humanity by an international military tribunal.', 'the historical context of the UDHR', 'Nuremberg established that individuals, including state officials, can be held accountable for atrocities, a principle that shaped the post-war human rights order')],
    apply=[('Identify which UDHR article is most relevant to each situation: (a) a journalist jailed for criticising the government, (b) a person detained without trial, (c) a refugee fleeing persecution.', '(a) Art. 19 – freedom of opinion and expression; (b) Art. 9 – no arbitrary arrest/detention (also Art. 10 fair hearing); (c) Art. 14 – right to seek asylum. 1 mark each.', 3),
           ('Outline two features of the post-1945 international context that explain why the UDHR was adopted in 1948.', 'The shock of WWII and the Holocaust; the creation of the UN with a Charter referring to human rights (Art. 1(3)); the onset of the Cold War requiring compromise; emerging decolonisation. 2 marks each.', 4)],
    sources=[('"Where, after all, do universal human rights begin? In small places, close to home – so close and so small that they cannot be seen on any maps of the world."', 'Eleanor Roosevelt, speech at the United Nations, 1958', 'With reference to the source, explain Eleanor Roosevelt\'s view of human rights.', 'Human rights must be realised in everyday local settings – homes, schools, workplaces – not only in international documents; universal rights are meaningful only when experienced locally.'),
             ('"Whereas disregard and contempt for human rights have resulted in barbarous acts which have outraged the conscience of mankind..."', 'Preamble to the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, 1948', 'Explain, with reference to the source, the historical context in which the UDHR was adopted.', 'The "barbarous acts" refer to the Second World War and the Holocaust; the UDHR was a response intended to prevent such atrocities by setting universal standards.')],
    evaluate=[('The UDHR is a Western document that does not reflect global values.', ['drafted in a UN dominated by Western states in 1948', 'emphasis on individual rights', 'many colonised peoples unrepresented'],
               ['drafters from China, Lebanon, India, Chile', 'includes socio-economic rights', 'adopted by states from many regions and reaffirmed in Vienna 1993 by 171 states'],
               'The UDHR reflects its time and Western influence, but its drafting and later adoption were more global than critics suggest.'),
              ('The UDHR has been the most important development in the history of human rights.', ['foundational for all later treaties', 'universal reach', 'cited in national constitutions'],
               ['non-binding', 'binding covenants and courts (ICC, ECtHR) arguably more important', 'abolition of slavery and decolonisation earlier or parallel'],
               'The UDHR is foundational, but its importance lies in what it inspired; binding treaties and enforcement mechanisms made rights effective.')],
    extended=[('Analyse how the historical context of 1945–1948 shaped the content of the UDHR.', 'Discuss WWII and Holocaust (life, dignity, prohibition of torture), Cold War compromise (both civil and socio-economic rights), sovereignty concerns (non-binding), colonial context (limited representation; later decolonisation). Evaluate the legacy.', 8, 'long')],
))

