R.facts('ch08-st1', F(
    terms=[('ICCPR', 'the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (1966, in force 1976), monitored by the Human Rights Committee'),
           ('ICESCR', 'the International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights (1966, in force 1976), monitored by the Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights'),
           ('CEDAW', 'the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women (1979), sometimes called an international bill of rights for women'),
           ('CRC', 'the Convention on the Rights of the Child (1989), the most widely ratified human rights treaty'),
           ('reservation', 'a statement by a state, when ratifying a treaty, that excludes or modifies the legal effect of certain provisions for that state'),
           ('ratification', 'the formal act by which a state consents to be legally bound by a treaty')],
    facts=[('Which of the following is the only UN member state that has not ratified the Convention on the Rights of the Child?', 'The United States', ['China', 'India', 'Saudi Arabia'], 'The US signed in 1995 but has not ratified.'),
           ('Which of the following treaties did the United States sign in 1980 but never ratify?', 'CEDAW', ['The ICCPR', 'The Convention against Torture', 'The Genocide Convention'], 'The US ratified the ICCPR in 1992.'),
           ('Which of the following bodies monitors implementation of the ICCPR?', 'The Human Rights Committee', ['The Human Rights Council', 'The Security Council', 'The International Criminal Court'], 'It reviews state reports and, under the First Optional Protocol, individual complaints.'),
           ('Which of the following years did both international covenants enter into force?', '1976', ['1948', '1966', '1993'], 'They were adopted in 1966 and entered into force in 1976 after 35 ratifications.')],
    why=[('Explain why states attach reservations to human rights treaties.', 'Reservations allow states to join a treaty while excluding provisions that conflict with their constitution, religion or culture – e.g. several states have reservations to CEDAW Article 16 on equality in marriage and family law; critics argue some reservations undermine the treaty\'s purpose.'),
         ('Explain why ratification of a treaty does not guarantee implementation.', 'Treaty bodies cannot enforce decisions; implementation depends on domestic laws, courts, resources and political will, so states may ratify for reputational reasons while violating the treaty in practice.'),
         ('Explain why the Convention on the Rights of the Child gained near-universal ratification.', 'Children\'s protection is widely seen as uncontroversial, the treaty combines civil and socio-economic rights, and states face little cost to joining; advocacy by UNICEF and NGOs also helped.')],
    contrast=[('signing a treaty', 'ratifying a treaty', 'Signing shows intention to be bound and an obligation not to defeat the treaty\'s purpose; ratifying is the formal consent to be legally bound, usually after domestic approval (e.g. US Senate consent).'),
              ('the ICCPR', 'the ICESCR', 'The ICCPR covers civil and political rights requiring immediate respect (e.g. no torture, free expression); the ICESCR covers economic, social and cultural rights (work, health, education) realised progressively.')],
    misc=[('Once a state ratifies CEDAW, discrimination against women in that state ends.', 'Ratification creates legal obligations and reporting duties, but discrimination often persists; implementation depends on domestic law and practice, and the CEDAW Committee can only make recommendations.'),
          ('The Human Rights Committee and the Human Rights Council are the same body.', 'The Human Rights Committee is an expert treaty body monitoring the ICCPR; the Human Rights Council is an intergovernmental body of 47 states created in 2006.')],
    cases=[('Afghanistan and CEDAW under the Taliban', 'Afghanistan ratified CEDAW in 2003; since 2021 the Taliban have barred girls from secondary school and women from universities and most jobs.', 'the gap between ratification and implementation', 'treaty obligations remain legally in place, but a regime that rejects them makes them unenforceable'),
           ('Rwanda and women\'s representation', 'Rwanda\'s 2003 constitution set a 30% minimum quota for women in decision-making bodies; women now hold over 60% of seats in the lower house.', 'implementation of CEDAW principles', 'constitutional measures turned treaty commitments into strong outcomes for political representation'),
           ('Toonen v Australia (1994)', 'The UN Human Rights Committee found that Tasmanian laws criminalising sex between men violated the right to privacy under the ICCPR; Tasmania repealed the laws in 1997.', 'how treaty bodies influence states', 'an individual complaint led to a finding that prompted domestic legal change, despite non-binding status'),
           ('the CRC and child labour', 'The CRC (Art. 32) protects children from economic exploitation; the ILO estimated 160 million children were in child labour in 2020.', 'limits of treaty implementation', 'near-universal ratification has not ended child labour, showing that poverty and weak enforcement limit treaty impact')],
    apply=[('A state ratifies CEDAW but enters a reservation to Article 2 (the obligation to eliminate discrimination in law and policy). Explain why other states and the CEDAW Committee might object.', 'Article 2 is central to the object and purpose of the treaty; a reservation incompatible with the object and purpose is not permitted under the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties (Art. 19); it would hollow out the commitment.', 3),
           ('Identify the treaty most relevant to each case: (a) a girl forced into marriage at 14, (b) a journalist detained without charge, (c) a government cutting public health spending sharply.', '(a) CRC and CEDAW (Art. 16); (b) ICCPR (Arts. 9 and 19); (c) ICESCR (Art. 12, non-retrogression). 1 mark each.', 3)],
    sources=[('"Our country has ratified every major treaty. The problem is not the law in Geneva; it is the police station in our district."', 'Interview with a human rights lawyer in South Asia, 2020 (adapted)', 'With reference to the source, explain the challenge of implementing human rights treaties.', 'Ratification is formal, but violations occur locally (police abuse); treaties depend on domestic institutions and enforcement, which may be weak or unwilling.'),
             ('"The Committee notes with concern that the State party maintains reservations to articles 9 and 16, and urges it to withdraw them."', 'Typical concluding observation of the CEDAW Committee (adapted)', 'Explain, with reference to the source, how treaty bodies monitor states.', 'Treaty bodies review periodic state reports and issue concluding observations that identify concerns and recommend action; they cannot compel, but they apply pressure through dialogue and publicity.')],
    evaluate=[('International human rights treaties have little effect on how states behave.', ['ratification without implementation (Afghanistan, child labour)', 'no enforcement', 'reservations'],
               ['legal change after treaty body findings (Toonen)', 'domestic courts apply treaties', 'civil society uses treaties (Simmons, Mobilizing for Human Rights)'],
               'Treaties rarely change behaviour on their own, but they give domestic actors tools to press for change, especially in democracies.'),
              ('The refusal of the United States to ratify key treaties undermines the international human rights system.', ['sets precedent of selectivity', 'weakens US criticism of others'],
               ['US protects many rights domestically', 'system has near-universal participation without the US', 'others also violate treaties they ratified'],
               'US non-ratification weakens the system\'s legitimacy and US credibility, but the system\'s survival depends far more on broader state practice.')],
    extended=[('Analyse the effectiveness of one human rights treaty in a country you have studied.', 'Choose a treaty (CEDAW, CRC, CAT) and a state; assess legal changes, institutions, reservations, reports, NGO use, and outcomes, giving a balanced judgement.', 8, 'long')],
))

R.facts('ch08-st2', F(
    terms=[('UN Human Rights Council', 'an intergovernmental body of 47 member states, created in 2006 to replace the Commission on Human Rights, which promotes and protects human rights'),
           ('Universal Periodic Review', 'the Human Rights Council process, begun in 2008, in which every UN member\'s human rights record is reviewed by other states roughly every four and a half years'),
           ('International Criminal Court (ICC)', 'a permanent court in The Hague created by the Rome Statute (1998, in force 2002) that tries individuals for genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes and aggression'),
           ('complementarity', 'the principle that the ICC acts only when national courts are unwilling or unable genuinely to investigate or prosecute'),
           ('European Court of Human Rights', 'the court in Strasbourg that enforces the European Convention on Human Rights for the 46 member states of the Council of Europe'),
           ('special rapporteur', 'an independent expert appointed by the Human Rights Council to report on a country or a thematic issue')],
    facts=[('Which of the following leaders was the subject of an ICC arrest warrant in March 2023 for the unlawful deportation of children from Ukraine?', 'Vladimir Putin', ['Alexander Lukashenko', 'Recep Tayyip Erdoğan', 'Xi Jinping'], 'The warrant also named Russia\'s children\'s rights commissioner Maria Lvova-Belova.'),
           ('Which of the following former heads of state was arrested in March 2025 and transferred to the ICC in The Hague?', 'Rodrigo Duterte of the Philippines', ['Omar al-Bashir of Sudan', 'Jair Bolsonaro of Brazil', 'Laurent Gbagbo of Côte d\'Ivoire'], 'He faces charges of crimes against humanity linked to his "war on drugs".'),
           ('Which of the following crimes was added to the ICC\'s active jurisdiction in 2018?', 'The crime of aggression', ['Genocide', 'Piracy', 'Terrorism'], 'The Kampala amendments (2010) were activated in 2018.'),
           ('Which of the following countries left the Council of Europe in 2022 and ceased to be party to the European Convention on Human Rights?', 'Russia', ['Türkiye', 'Hungary', 'Azerbaijan'], 'Russia was expelled after its invasion of Ukraine.')],
    why=[('Explain why the ICC has been accused of bias against Africa.', 'Most of its early cases and convictions concerned African defendants (e.g. DRC, Uganda, Sudan, Kenya), while powerful non-members (US, China, Russia) were beyond reach; several cases were Security Council or self-referrals. Recent investigations (Ukraine, Palestine, Philippines, Venezuela, Afghanistan) have broadened its geographic focus.'),
         ('Explain why the Human Rights Council replaced the Commission on Human Rights.', 'The Commission was discredited because states with poor records joined it to shield themselves and others; the Council introduced the Universal Periodic Review, competitive election by the General Assembly and the possibility of suspending members (Libya 2011, Russia 2022).'),
         ('Explain why regional courts are often more effective than global mechanisms.', 'Regional courts like the ECtHR issue binding judgments, have established compliance procedures (Committee of Ministers), and operate among states with shared values and institutions, though compliance still varies.')],
    contrast=[('the International Criminal Court', 'the European Court of Human Rights', 'The ICC prosecutes individuals for international crimes and can impose prison sentences; the ECtHR judges whether states have violated the European Convention and can order compensation or changes in law.'),
              ('the Universal Periodic Review', 'treaty body review', 'The UPR is a peer review by states of all UN members\' human rights records, regardless of treaty membership; treaty body review is conducted by independent experts and applies only to states that have ratified that treaty.')],
    misc=[('The ICC can try any person in the world for any crime.', 'The ICC tries only genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes and aggression, normally where crimes occur on the territory of a state party, by its nationals, or after a Security Council referral – and only when national courts are unwilling or unable.'),
          ('The Human Rights Council is a court that can punish states.', 'The Council is a political body of states; it can adopt resolutions, create commissions of inquiry and appoint rapporteurs, but it cannot issue binding judgments or penalties.')],
    cases=[('the ICC arrest warrants for Benjamin Netanyahu and Yoav Gallant (November 2024)', 'The ICC issued warrants for the Israeli prime minister and former defence minister for alleged war crimes and crimes against humanity in Gaza; the US imposed sanctions on ICC officials in 2025.', 'the politics of international criminal justice', 'the case shows the ICC acting against leaders allied with powerful states, and the political backlash that limits enforcement'),
           ('the Mongolian visit of Vladimir Putin (2024)', 'Putin visited Mongolia, an ICC state party, in September 2024 and was not arrested; the ICC later found Mongolia had failed to comply with its obligations.', 'the enforcement problem of the ICC', 'the ICC depends on states to arrest suspects and has no police force, so warrants may not be executed'),
           ('Hirst v United Kingdom (2005)', 'The ECtHR ruled that the UK\'s blanket ban on prisoners voting breached the Convention; the UK resisted for years before making a limited change in 2017.', 'the limits of regional courts', 'even a democracy with a strong rule of law delayed compliance, showing tension between sovereignty and supranational courts'),
           ('the conviction of Dominic Ongwen (2021)', 'The ICC convicted the former Lord\'s Resistance Army commander of war crimes and crimes against humanity in northern Uganda and sentenced him to 25 years.', 'individual criminal accountability', 'a perpetrator who was himself abducted as a child was held responsible, illustrating both the reach and the complexity of international justice')],
    apply=[('A militia leader commits mass killings in a country that is not an ICC state party. Identify two ways the ICC could still gain jurisdiction.', 'A UN Security Council referral under Chapter VII (as with Darfur 2005, Libya 2011); the state accepting jurisdiction by a declaration under Art. 12(3) (as Ukraine did before joining); or crimes committed partly on a state party\'s territory (Bangladesh/Myanmar). 2 marks each.', 4),
           ('Identify the mechanism most appropriate for each: (a) a Kenyan citizen claiming a violation of the African Charter, (b) a review of Saudi Arabia\'s human rights record by other states, (c) prosecution of a commander for war crimes.', '(a) African Commission/African Court on Human and Peoples\' Rights; (b) Universal Periodic Review; (c) ICC (or national courts first under complementarity). 1 mark each.', 3)],
    sources=[('"Justice that depends on the cooperation of the very states whose leaders are accused is justice that waits a very long time."', 'Commentary on international criminal justice, 2024 (adapted)', 'With reference to the source, explain one weakness of the ICC.', 'The ICC has no police force and relies on states to arrest and surrender suspects; when states protect accused leaders (e.g. Putin, al-Bashir), cases stall.'),
             ('"Every country, from the most powerful to the smallest, sits in the same room every few years and answers questions about its record."', 'UN official describing the Universal Periodic Review, 2022 (adapted)', 'Explain, with reference to the source, one strength of the Universal Periodic Review.', 'Universality: all 193 states are reviewed equally, so no state is singled out and even powerful states must present and defend their records, encouraging dialogue and recommendations.')],
    evaluate=[('The International Criminal Court has failed to deliver justice.', ['few convictions since 2002 (around a dozen)', 'cannot arrest powerful suspects', 'non-members US, China, Russia, India', 'alleged African bias'],
               ['deterrence and stigma', 'convictions (Lubanga, Ongwen)', 'Duterte transfer 2025', 'investigations now worldwide'],
               'The ICC has delivered limited justice because it depends on state cooperation, but it has established accountability norms and occasional landmark cases.'),
              ('Regional human rights courts protect rights more effectively than UN mechanisms.', ['binding judgments (ECtHR, IACtHR)', 'higher compliance in Europe'],
               ['regional systems absent in Asia', 'non-compliance (Russia, Türkiye, UK on prisoner voting)', 'UN mechanisms are universal'],
               'Regional courts are generally more effective where they exist, but coverage is uneven and they depend on state goodwill.')],
    extended=[('Evaluate the effectiveness of one human rights monitoring or enforcement mechanism you have studied.', 'Choose ICC, HRC/UPR, a treaty body, ECtHR, IACtHR or African Court; use criteria (reach, compliance, independence, legitimacy) and specific cases.', 8, 'long')],
))

R.facts('ch08-st3', F(
    terms=[('naming and shaming', 'the strategy of publicly exposing human rights violations to damage a state\'s or company\'s reputation and pressure it to change'),
           ('civil society', 'the space of voluntary associations, NGOs, movements and media between the state, the market and the family'),
           ('advocacy', 'public campaigning and lobbying to influence policy, law or public opinion'),
           ('prisoner of conscience', 'Amnesty International\'s term for a person imprisoned for their beliefs or identity who has not used or advocated violence'),
           ('open-source investigation', 'research using publicly available data such as satellite images, videos and social media to document violations')],
    facts=[('Which of the following NGOs was founded in 1961 after Peter Benenson\'s article "The Forgotten Prisoners"?', 'Amnesty International', ['Human Rights Watch', 'Oxfam', 'Greenpeace'], 'Amnesty won the 1977 Nobel Peace Prize.'),
           ('Which of the following organizations began in 1978 as Helsinki Watch?', 'Human Rights Watch', ['Amnesty International', 'Freedom House', 'Article 19'], 'It monitored Soviet bloc compliance with the 1975 Helsinki Accords.'),
           ('Which of the following Russian human rights organizations was forcibly dissolved in 2021–2022 and co-awarded the 2022 Nobel Peace Prize?', 'Memorial', ['Amnesty Russia', 'Golos', 'Pussy Riot'], 'Memorial documented Soviet repression and recent abuses.'),
           ('Which of the following describes the role of Bellingcat?', 'An investigative collective using open-source information to document conflicts and abuses', ['A UN agency for refugees', 'An intergovernmental court', 'A state intelligence service'], 'It identified suspects in the MH17 downing over Ukraine (2014).')],
    why=[('Explain why NGOs\' independence matters for their influence.', 'Their credibility depends on being seen as impartial and evidence-based; if they accept government funding or are seen as politically aligned, targeted states dismiss their reports as propaganda.'),
         ('Explain why many governments have introduced "foreign agent" laws restricting NGOs.', 'Such laws (e.g. Russia 2012, Georgia 2024, Hungary) label foreign-funded NGOs as agents, increasing reporting burdens and stigma; governments justify them as protecting sovereignty, but critics see them as attempts to silence independent monitoring.'),
         ('Explain why NGOs are important in human rights monitoring even where courts exist.', 'They gather evidence and testimony, submit shadow reports to treaty bodies, bring litigation, inform media and diplomats, and can act faster and more independently than intergovernmental bodies.')],
    contrast=[('Amnesty International', 'Human Rights Watch', 'Amnesty is a mass-membership movement with millions of supporters that campaigns through letter-writing and mobilisation; Human Rights Watch is a research-based organization with professional staff that focuses on detailed reports and advocacy with governments.'),
              ('an NGO shadow report', 'a state report to a treaty body', 'A state report presents the government\'s account of implementation; an NGO shadow report gives civil society\'s independent assessment, often highlighting gaps the state omits.')],
    misc=[('Human rights NGOs have legal powers to punish governments.', 'NGOs have no legal enforcement power; they influence through information, publicity, litigation support and lobbying.'),
          ('All NGOs share the same values and priorities.', 'NGOs differ widely in mandates, funding and politics – some focus on civil rights, others on economic rights, religion or conservative causes – and they often disagree.')],
    cases=[('Human Rights Watch reporting on Xinjiang', 'HRW and Amnesty documented mass detention of Uyghurs; in 2022 the UN Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights reported "serious human rights violations" that may amount to crimes against humanity.', 'NGO monitoring influencing UN action', 'NGO evidence built pressure that contributed to an official UN assessment despite China\'s denial'),
           ('the Mothers of the Plaza de Mayo in Argentina', 'From 1977, mothers of people "disappeared" by the military junta marched weekly in Buenos Aires, demanding information and justice; after democracy returned, trials of junta leaders followed.', 'civil society and accountability', 'grassroots action kept violations visible and sustained demands for justice over decades'),
           ('Memorial in Russia', 'Memorial documented Soviet-era repression and abuses in Chechnya; Russian courts ordered its dissolution in 2021 for alleged breaches of the "foreign agent" law, and it shared the 2022 Nobel Peace Prize.', 'state restrictions on civil society', 'a monitoring NGO was shut down by the state, illustrating both its importance and its vulnerability'),
           ('Amnesty International\'s 2022 apartheid report on Israel', 'Amnesty concluded that Israel maintained a system of apartheid against Palestinians; Israel and several governments rejected the report, while others cited it.', 'contested NGO legitimacy', 'the report demonstrated NGO agenda-setting power but also how NGOs\' legitimacy is contested in politicised conflicts')],
    apply=[('Identify three strategies human rights NGOs use and give one example of each.', 'Research reports (HRW on Xinjiang); mass campaigns/letter-writing (Amnesty\'s "Write for Rights"); strategic litigation (ECtHR cases); shadow reports to treaty bodies; open-source investigation (Bellingcat on MH17). 1 mark each.', 3),
           ('A government passes a law requiring NGOs receiving foreign funds to register as "foreign agents". Explain two likely effects on human rights monitoring.', 'Chilling effect and self-censorship; loss of funding and closure (Memorial); reduced public trust due to stigma; reduced reporting to UN bodies. 2 marks each.', 4)],
    sources=[('"We cannot arrest anyone. Our only weapons are facts and the courage of witnesses who tell us what happened."', 'Researcher at an international human rights NGO, 2023 (adapted)', 'Identify the sources of NGO power suggested in the source and explain one.', 'Information/facts and witness testimony; facts give credibility and allow naming and shaming, influencing governments, media and international bodies.'),
             ('"Those organizations take money from foreign governments and then tell us how to run our country."', 'Statement by a government spokesperson, 2024 (adapted)', 'With reference to the source, explain one criticism made of human rights NGOs.', 'They are accused of lacking independence and legitimacy because of foreign funding, and of interfering in sovereign affairs – a sovereignty-based critique often used to justify restrictions.')],
    evaluate=[('Non-governmental organizations are the most effective actors in protecting human rights.', ['independent monitoring and evidence', 'agenda-setting (landmines, ICC creation)', 'Mothers of Plaza de Mayo'],
               ['no enforcement power', 'restricted by states (Memorial)', 'states and courts make binding decisions'],
               'NGOs are indispensable for exposing violations and mobilising pressure, but they rely on states and courts to secure lasting protection.'),
              ('Naming and shaming changes the behaviour of human rights violators.', ['reputational costs and aid conditions', 'release of prisoners after campaigns'],
               ['powerful states ignore criticism (China on Xinjiang)', 'can provoke backlash or more repression'],
               'Naming and shaming works best when targets value their reputation or depend on outside support; it is weakest against powerful or isolated regimes.')],
    extended=[('Analyse the role of civil society in monitoring human rights in one country you have studied.', 'Identify specific organizations, methods (documentation, litigation, campaigns), state responses (foreign-agent laws), international links (boomerang pattern), and evaluate impact.', 8, 'long')],
))

R.facts('ch08-st4', F(
    terms=[('Responsibility to Protect (R2P)', 'the principle, endorsed at the 2005 World Summit, that states must protect populations from genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing and crimes against humanity, and that the international community should act when a state manifestly fails'),
           ('sovereignty as responsibility', 'the idea, developed by Francis Deng and the ICISS, that sovereignty entails a duty to protect citizens rather than only a right to non-interference'),
           ('Pillar III of R2P', 'the responsibility of the international community to take timely and decisive collective action, through the Security Council, when a state manifestly fails to protect its population'),
           ('Responsibility while Protecting', 'Brazil\'s 2011 proposal that interventions under R2P should be subject to stricter criteria and monitoring, after concerns about Libya'),
           ('ICISS', 'the International Commission on Intervention and State Sovereignty, sponsored by Canada, whose 2001 report introduced the term Responsibility to Protect')],
    facts=[('Which of the following UN Security Council resolutions authorised "all necessary measures" to protect civilians in Libya in March 2011?', 'Resolution 1973', ['Resolution 1325', 'Resolution 2254', 'Resolution 678'], 'Brazil, China, Germany, India and Russia abstained.'),
           ('Which of the following paragraphs of the 2005 World Summit Outcome Document set out R2P?', 'Paragraphs 138 and 139', ['Articles 2(4) and 51', 'Paragraphs 1 to 3', 'Article 4(h)'], 'They limit R2P to four crimes and to action through the Security Council.'),
           ('Which of the following crimes is NOT one of the four covered by R2P?', 'Terrorism', ['Genocide', 'Ethnic cleansing', 'Crimes against humanity'], 'R2P covers genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing and crimes against humanity.'),
           ('Which of the following UN Secretaries-General set out the three pillars of R2P in a 2009 report?', 'Ban Ki-moon', ['Kofi Annan', 'António Guterres', 'Boutros Boutros-Ghali'], 'Implementing the Responsibility to Protect (2009).')],
    why=[('Explain why the Libya intervention damaged support for R2P.', 'Resolution 1973 authorised protection of civilians, but NATO\'s campaign contributed to the overthrow and death of Gaddafi; Russia, China and others felt R2P had been used for regime change, making them less willing to authorise action in Syria.'),
         ('Explain why R2P was designed to work through the UN Security Council.', 'To gain wide acceptance in 2005, states limited collective action to Security Council authorisation under Chapter VII, reassuring those who feared unilateral intervention by powerful states, though this also means the veto can block action.'),
         ('Explain why R2P emerged in the early 2000s.', 'The failures to prevent genocide in Rwanda (1994) and Srebrenica (1995), and the controversy over NATO\'s unauthorised Kosovo intervention (1999), led to a search for principles reconciling sovereignty with protection (ICISS 2001).')],
    contrast=[('R2P', 'humanitarian intervention', 'Humanitarian intervention refers to the use of force without consent to stop atrocities, often unilaterally; R2P is a broader framework emphasising prevention and state responsibility first, with force only as a last resort authorised by the Security Council.'),
              ('Pillar I of R2P', 'Pillar III of R2P', 'Pillar I is each state\'s own responsibility to protect its population; Pillar III is the international community\'s responsibility to take collective action when a state manifestly fails.')],
    misc=[('The Responsibility to Protect means the international community must use military force whenever human rights are violated.', 'R2P covers only four mass atrocity crimes and emphasises prevention, assistance and peaceful measures; force is a last resort that requires Security Council authorisation.'),
          ('The Responsibility to Protect is a binding treaty obligation.', 'R2P is a political commitment from the 2005 World Summit Outcome Document, not a treaty; it builds on existing law such as the Genocide Convention.')],
    cases=[('the 2011 Libya intervention', 'Resolution 1973 authorised a no-fly zone and protection of civilians in Benghazi; the NATO-led campaign ended with the fall of Gaddafi and years of instability.', 'the application and criticism of R2P', 'R2P was invoked to authorise force, but the shift to regime change produced lasting mistrust'),
           ('Syria (2011 onwards)', 'Despite massive civilian casualties and chemical attacks, Russia and China vetoed Security Council resolutions; no R2P-based intervention took place.', 'the selectivity of R2P', 'the veto and the post-Libya backlash prevented collective action, revealing the limits of Pillar III'),
           ('Kenya after the 2007–2008 election violence', 'After violence killed over 1,100 people, mediation led by Kofi Annan produced a power-sharing agreement, often cited as a successful non-military application of R2P.', 'preventive and diplomatic R2P', 'R2P\'s preventive pillar worked through diplomacy rather than force'),
           ('the Rohingya crisis in Myanmar (2017)', 'Military "clearance operations" drove over 700,000 Rohingya into Bangladesh; a UN fact-finding mission called for genocide investigations, but the Security Council took no coercive action.', 'the failure of R2P', 'China\'s and Russia\'s protection of Myanmar meant the international community did not act despite mass atrocities')],
    apply=[('A government is massacring members of an ethnic minority and the Security Council is deadlocked by a veto. Identify two options available under or alongside R2P and explain one limitation of each.', 'General Assembly "Uniting for Peace" resolution (limitation: non-binding); regional action, e.g. AU Art. 4(h) (limitation: capacity, legal debate without SC authorisation); targeted sanctions by coalitions; ICC referral (needs SC unless state party); humanitarian corridors. 2 marks each.', 4),
           ('Explain how the concept of "sovereignty as responsibility" changes the traditional Westphalian view.', 'Westphalian sovereignty emphasises non-interference; sovereignty as responsibility says sovereignty is conditional on protecting the population, so a state that commits atrocities forfeits its protection against intervention.', 3)],
    sources=[('"We authorised the protection of civilians, not a change of regime. Libya taught us that R2P can be a Trojan horse."', 'Statement attributed to a BRICS diplomat at the UN, 2012 (adapted)', 'With reference to the source, explain a criticism of R2P.', 'States that supported or abstained on Resolution 1973 felt NATO exceeded its mandate by pursuing regime change, so R2P was seen as a pretext for Western interests – a "Trojan horse".'),
             ('"Sovereignty no longer exclusively protects states from foreign interference; it is a charge of responsibility that holds states accountable for the welfare of their people."', 'Paraphrase of the ICISS report The Responsibility to Protect, 2001', 'Explain, with reference to the source, the idea of sovereignty as responsibility.', 'Sovereignty is redefined from a shield against interference to a duty to protect people; if a state fails, its claim to non-interference weakens.')],
    evaluate=[('The Responsibility to Protect has failed to protect civilians from mass atrocities.', ['Syria, Myanmar, Sudan (Darfur, El Fasher 2025), Gaza', 'veto blocks Pillar III', 'Libya backlash'],
               ['prevention success in Kenya 2008', 'normative shift: states accept responsibility in principle', 'Côte d\'Ivoire 2011'],
               'R2P has reshaped norms but has rarely protected civilians when great powers disagree, so its practical record is weak.'),
              ('The Responsibility to Protect is a tool used by powerful states to justify intervention in weaker states.', ['Libya regime change', 'selective application'],
               ['R2P restricts unilateral intervention by requiring SC approval', 'African Union supported similar principles', 'prevention focus'],
               'R2P can be misused, but its design actually limits unilateralism; selectivity reflects great-power politics more than the norm itself.')],
    extended=[('Compare two cases where R2P was or was not applied and explain the difference in international response.', 'E.g. Libya 2011 (applied – regional support from Arab League, Gaddafi\'s threats, no P5 veto) vs Syria (not applied – Russian alliance, post-Libya distrust). Analyse interests, legitimacy and institutional factors.', 8, 'long')],
))

P1['ch08'] = dict(
    src=[('"Since 2002 the Court has issued dozens of arrest warrants, but most suspects remain at large. It has no police, no army, and depends entirely on states to make arrests."',
          'Report on the International Criminal Court, 2024 (adapted)',
          'Identify three points made about the ICC in {S}.',
          ['it has issued dozens of arrest warrants', 'most suspects remain at large', 'it has no police or army and depends on states to make arrests'],
          'Explain, with reference to {S}, why enforcement is a challenge for international courts.',
          'International courts lack coercive capacity; they rely on state cooperation, which may be withheld for political reasons, so warrants often go unexecuted.'),
         ('"Our shadow report showed the Committee what the government\'s report left out: the women still being married at thirteen in our northern provinces."',
          'Director of a women\'s rights NGO, 2022 (adapted)',
          'Identify three things the NGO did or revealed according to {S}.',
          ['produced a shadow report', 'showed what the government\'s report left out', 'revealed child marriage at thirteen in northern provinces'],
          'Explain, with reference to {S}, how NGOs contribute to monitoring human rights treaties.',
          'NGOs provide independent information to treaty bodies (CEDAW Committee) through shadow reports, exposing gaps in official reporting and enabling targeted recommendations.'),
         ('"In Libya the Council acted within days. In Syria it did nothing for years. The principle is the same; only the politics differ."',
          'Commentary on the Responsibility to Protect, 2016 (adapted)',
          'Identify three claims made about R2P in {S}.',
          ['the Council acted within days in Libya', 'it did nothing for years in Syria', 'the principle is the same but politics differ'],
          'Explain, with reference to {S}, why R2P is applied selectively.',
          'Action under Pillar III depends on Security Council votes; in Syria, Russia and China (allies of the regime and distrustful after Libya) vetoed action, so geopolitics determines when R2P is applied.'),
         ('"Every state is reviewed. Every state receives recommendations. Many accept them – and some even implement them."',
          'Briefing on the Universal Periodic Review, 2023 (adapted)',
          'Identify three features of the UPR mentioned in {S}.',
          ['every state is reviewed', 'every state receives recommendations', 'many accept and some implement them'],
          'Explain, with reference to {S}, one strength and one limitation of the UPR.',
          'Strength: universality and equal treatment of all states. Limitation: implementation is voluntary – recommendations are accepted but not always acted upon.')],
    cmp=[(0, 2, 'the effectiveness of international responses to atrocities', ['both show international mechanisms depend on states', 'both indicate inconsistent outcomes'],
          ['Source A focuses on a court and arrests; Source C on the Security Council and intervention', 'A emphasises lack of enforcement capacity; C emphasises political selectivity']),
         (1, 3, 'how human rights are monitored', ['both describe reporting processes', 'both suggest monitoring can lead to recommendations'],
          ['Source B shows civil society exposing state omissions; Source D shows peer review among states', 'B stresses gaps in state reporting; D is more positive about state engagement'])],
    ev=[(0, 2, 'international mechanisms have been ineffective in protecting human rights.', ['Source A: suspects at large', 'Source C: Syria inaction', 'own knowledge: Myanmar, Sudan'],
         ['Source C: Libya action', 'own knowledge: Ongwen and Duterte cases, Kenya 2008']),
        (1, 3, 'non-state actors are more important than states in monitoring human rights.', ['Source B: shadow reports', 'own knowledge: HRW on Xinjiang, Bellingcat'],
         ['Source D: state peer review', 'own knowledge: states create courts and treaties; NGOs restricted (Memorial)'])],
)

# =============================================================================================
# ch09 Rights and Justice – debates: universalism, relativism and justice
# =============================================================================================
R.facts('ch09-st1', F(
    terms=[('universalism', 'the view that human rights apply equally to all people everywhere, regardless of culture, religion or political system'),
           ('cultural relativism', 'the view that rights and moral values are shaped by culture and should be understood and judged within their cultural context'),
           ('Asian values', 'a 1990s argument by leaders such as Lee Kuan Yew and Mahathir Mohamad that Asian societies prioritise community, order and duty over individual rights'),
           ('cultural imperialism', 'the imposition of one culture\'s values, practices or institutions on other societies, often by more powerful states'),
           ('overlapping consensus', 'agreement on core principles reached by people who hold different comprehensive moral or religious views (John Rawls), sometimes used to defend universal rights')],
    facts=[('Which of the following 1993 documents, adopted by Asian states, stressed national and regional particularities in human rights?', 'The Bangkok Declaration', ['The Vienna Declaration', 'The Banjul Charter', 'The Cairo Declaration'], 'It was adopted before the Vienna World Conference on Human Rights.'),
           ('Which of the following declared in 1993 that "all human rights are universal, indivisible and interdependent and interrelated"?', 'The Vienna Declaration and Programme of Action', ['The Bangkok Declaration', 'The UN Charter', 'The Universal Declaration of Human Rights'], 'Adopted at the World Conference on Human Rights by 171 states.'),
           ('Which of the following leaders of Singapore was a prominent advocate of "Asian values"?', 'Lee Kuan Yew', ['Aung San Suu Kyi', 'Kim Dae-jung', 'Jacinda Ardern'], 'Kim Dae-jung argued against the thesis in Foreign Affairs (1994).'),
           ('Which of the following 1990 documents set out human rights within the framework of Islamic Sharia?', 'The Cairo Declaration on Human Rights in Islam', ['The Bangkok Declaration', 'The Vienna Declaration', 'The Yogyakarta Principles'], 'Adopted by the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation.')],
    why=[('Explain why some states use cultural relativism to reject international human rights criticism.', 'Relativist arguments allow governments to present criticism as Western interference in their culture and sovereignty, shielding practices such as restrictions on speech, LGBTQ+ rights or women\'s rights from external pressure.'),
         ('Explain why universalists argue that cultural relativism can be used to justify oppression.', 'Cultures are not uniform; elites often define "tradition" to preserve their power, and those harmed (e.g. women, minorities) may reject the practice – so relativism can silence dissenting voices within a culture.'),
         ('Explain why Amartya Sen rejected the Asian values thesis.', 'Sen argued that Asian traditions (e.g. Ashoka\'s tolerance, Buddhist and Confucian ideas) contain respect for freedom and that Western traditions were not always liberal; "Asia" is too diverse to have a single set of values.')],
    contrast=[('universalism', 'cultural relativism', 'Universalism holds that the same human rights apply to everyone everywhere because of shared humanity; cultural relativism holds that rights and values derive from cultural contexts and that outsiders should not impose their standards.'),
              ('strong cultural relativism', 'weak cultural relativism', 'Strong relativism treats culture as the main source of validity of rights, with universal standards only a check; weak relativism accepts universal rights as the main standard but allows cultural variation in how they are interpreted and implemented (Jack Donnelly).')],
    misc=[('Cultural relativism means that no practice can ever be criticised.', 'Weak relativism accepts universal minimum standards while allowing variation in interpretation; relativists can still criticise practices such as genocide or slavery.'),
          ('The idea of universal human rights was rejected by all non-Western states at Vienna in 1993.', 'At Vienna 171 states, including those that signed the Bangkok Declaration, affirmed that rights are universal, while noting national and regional particularities.')],
    cases=[('female genital mutilation (FGM)', 'UNICEF estimated in 2024 that over 230 million girls and women alive today have undergone FGM; many states, including Kenya (2011) and The Gambia (2015), have banned it, often led by local activists.', 'universalism versus cultural relativism', 'local campaigners framed the practice as a rights violation, showing universal norms can be championed from within cultures'),
           ('Uganda\'s Anti-Homosexuality Act (2023)', 'Uganda passed a law imposing severe penalties, including death for "aggravated homosexuality"; the World Bank suspended new lending and the US imposed visa restrictions.', 'conflict between universal rights and claims of cultural values', 'the government framed the law as protecting African values, while critics invoked universal rights to non-discrimination'),
           ('the Asian values debate in the 1990s', 'Lee Kuan Yew and Mahathir Mohamad argued that Asian societies valued order and community; Kim Dae-jung of South Korea responded that democracy was compatible with Asian traditions.', 'the relativist challenge to universal rights', 'the debate showed that claims about "culture" are contested within Asia itself'),
           ('France\'s ban on face coverings in public (2010)', 'France banned full-face veils in public places; the ECtHR upheld the ban in S.A.S. v France (2014), citing "living together", while the UN Human Rights Committee found a violation in 2018.', 'disagreement within universalist institutions', 'two human rights bodies interpreted the same universal rights differently, showing interpretation varies even in a universalist framework')],
    apply=[('A government argues that criticising its restrictions on media is "Western cultural imperialism". Outline a universalist response and a relativist response.', 'Universalist: freedom of expression (UDHR Art. 19, ICCPR Art. 19) applies to all; the state has ratified the ICCPR; local journalists themselves demand it. Relativist: values of social harmony and community may justify different balances; external pressure ignores local context. 2 marks each.', 4),
           ('Using Donnelly\'s distinction, classify this position: "Everyone has a right to family life, but what counts as a family can vary between societies." Explain.', 'Weak cultural relativism – the universal right is accepted, but its interpretation and implementation vary across cultures.', 2)],
    sources=[('"While human rights are universal in nature, they must be considered in the context of a dynamic and evolving process of international norm-setting, bearing in mind the significance of national and regional particularities."', 'Bangkok Declaration, Article 8, 1993', 'With reference to the source, explain how the Bangkok Declaration qualifies universalism.', 'It accepts that rights are universal but argues their application must consider national and regional particularities (culture, history, development), opening space for relativist interpretation.'),
             ('"Our grandmothers suffered in silence because they were told it was tradition. We are also part of our culture, and we say no."', 'Statement by a young activist against child marriage in West Africa, 2021 (adapted)', 'Explain, with reference to the source, a criticism of cultural relativism.', 'Cultures are internally contested; those affected may reject harmful traditions, so relativism can privilege elders\' or elites\' interpretations over the rights of vulnerable members.')],
    evaluate=[('Human rights are a Western concept that cannot be applied universally.', ['UDHR drafted in a Western-dominated UN', 'individualism', 'used selectively by Western powers'],
               ['non-Western drafters and traditions', 'Vienna 1993 reaffirmation', 'local movements demand rights (Sudan, Iran\'s "Woman, Life, Freedom")'],
               'Human rights have Western historical roots, but they have been adopted and reshaped by people worldwide, so they are universal in aspiration and contested in interpretation.'),
              ('Cultural relativism is mainly used by governments to avoid accountability.', ['Asian values and anti-LGBTQ laws used by authoritarian elites', 'shields repression'],
               ['genuine concerns about cultural imperialism', 'Indigenous peoples use relativist arguments to protect culture'],
               'Relativism is often invoked by governments to deflect criticism, but it also expresses legitimate concerns about imposition and cultural diversity.')],
    extended=[('Using two contrasting examples, evaluate the extent to which human rights can be both universal and culturally sensitive.', 'Use e.g. FGM campaigns led locally, Indigenous rights, face-veil bans, LGBTQ+ rights in Uganda; discuss weak relativism, overlapping consensus and local ownership; judge.', 8, 'long')],
))

R.facts('ch09-st2', F(
    terms=[('veil of ignorance', 'Rawls\'s thought experiment in which people choose principles of justice without knowing their own position in society'),
           ('difference principle', 'Rawls\'s principle that social and economic inequalities are just only if they benefit the least advantaged members of society'),
           ('utilitarianism', 'the theory, associated with Jeremy Bentham and John Stuart Mill, that the right action is the one that produces the greatest happiness for the greatest number'),
           ('libertarianism', 'a theory, associated with Robert Nozick, that prioritises individual liberty and property rights and limits the state to protecting them'),
           ('social justice', 'the fair distribution of wealth, opportunities and privileges within a society, often involving redistribution to reduce inequality'),
           ('entitlement theory', 'Nozick\'s theory that a distribution is just if holdings were acquired and transferred justly, whatever the resulting pattern')],
    facts=[('Which of the following wrote A Theory of Justice (1971)?', 'John Rawls', ['Robert Nozick', 'John Stuart Mill', 'Amartya Sen'], 'Rawls proposed justice as fairness.'),
           ('Which of the following works by Robert Nozick (1974) criticised redistributive theories of justice?', 'Anarchy, State, and Utopia', ['Utilitarianism', 'The Idea of Justice', 'Leviathan'], 'Nozick argued for a minimal state.'),
           ('Which of the following philosophers wrote that the "greatest happiness of the greatest number" is the measure of right and wrong?', 'Jeremy Bentham', ['John Locke', 'Immanuel Kant', 'John Rawls'], 'Bentham, A Fragment on Government (1776).'),
           ('Which of the following ideas is associated with Peter Singer\'s argument about global justice?', 'People in rich countries have a moral duty to give to prevent extreme poverty abroad', ['Taxation is equivalent to forced labour', 'Justice is the interest of the stronger', 'Only citizens have claims of justice'], 'Singer\'s "Famine, Affluence, and Morality" (1972) used the drowning child analogy.')],
    why=[('Explain why Rawls argued that people behind the veil of ignorance would choose the difference principle.', 'Not knowing whether they would be rich or poor, rational people would protect themselves against the worst outcome, so they would accept inequalities only if they improve the position of the least advantaged (maximin reasoning).'),
         ('Explain why Nozick argued that redistributive taxation can be unjust.', 'If people acquired their holdings justly (through fair acquisition and voluntary transfer), taking part of them by taxation violates their rights; Nozick compared taxation of earnings to forced labour.'),
         ('Explain why utilitarianism can conflict with individual rights.', 'If sacrificing a minority increases total happiness, utilitarian reasoning may justify it – e.g. torture to obtain information – whereas rights theories hold that some protections cannot be traded off.')],
    contrast=[('Rawls\'s theory of justice', 'Nozick\'s theory of justice', 'Rawls judges justice by the pattern of distribution, permitting redistribution to benefit the least advantaged; Nozick judges justice by the history of how holdings were acquired, rejecting patterned redistribution.'),
              ('distributive justice', 'retributive justice', 'Distributive justice concerns the fair allocation of resources and opportunities in society; retributive justice concerns fair punishment for wrongdoing, such as trials for war crimes.')],
    misc=[('Rawls believed that all inequality is unjust.', 'Rawls accepted inequalities if they benefit the least advantaged and are attached to positions open to all under fair equality of opportunity.'),
          ('Utilitarians only care about the happiness of the rich.', 'Utilitarianism counts each person\'s happiness equally; because of diminishing marginal utility, it often supports redistribution to the poor.')],
    cases=[('global vaccine distribution during COVID-19', 'High-income countries vaccinated most adults by late 2021, while low-income countries had vaccinated only a few per cent; WHO and COVAX called for fairer allocation.', 'theories of justice applied globally', 'a Rawlsian or utilitarian view would favour prioritising the most vulnerable worldwide, whereas nationalist positions prioritised citizens'),
           ('Nordic welfare states', 'Countries such as Denmark and Norway combine high taxation with universal health care, education and social security, and have low Gini coefficients.', 'social justice and redistribution', 'they illustrate a Rawlsian-style commitment to reducing inequality through redistribution while maintaining market economies'),
           ('South Africa\'s Truth and Reconciliation Commission (1996–2003)', 'The TRC offered amnesty to perpetrators who fully disclosed politically motivated crimes, prioritising truth and reconciliation over prosecution.', 'restorative versus retributive justice', 'it chose a restorative approach aimed at social healing, rather than punishment'),
           ('Estonia\'s flat income tax', 'Estonia introduced a flat income tax in 1994 and is often praised by free-market advocates for its simple, low-tax system.', 'libertarian ideas in policy', 'it reflects a preference for limited redistribution and individual economic freedom')],
    apply=[('A government must decide whether to spend a budget on (a) a hospital in the capital serving many people or (b) clinics for a remote, very poor minority. Explain how a utilitarian and a Rawlsian might decide.', 'Utilitarian: choose the option maximising total welfare – possibly (a) if it helps more people, though diminishing marginal utility could favour (b). Rawlsian: prioritise the least advantaged – (b). 2 marks each.', 4),
           ('Identify the theory of justice reflected in each statement: (a) "Taxing my wages to fund welfare is theft", (b) "Inequality is acceptable only if it improves the lot of the poorest", (c) "Choose the policy that produces the most happiness overall".', '(a) libertarianism (Nozick); (b) Rawls\'s difference principle; (c) utilitarianism. 1 mark each.', 3)],
    sources=[('"Behind the veil, none of us knows whether we will be born in Oslo or in a refugee camp. What rules would we choose then?"', 'Lecture on global justice, 2022 (adapted)', 'With reference to the source, explain how Rawls\'s veil of ignorance can be applied to global politics.', 'If people did not know their nationality or circumstances, they would choose global principles protecting the least advantaged (e.g. refugees), supporting cosmopolitan duties – though Rawls himself limited the difference principle to domestic societies.'),
             ('"What I earn through honest work and fair exchange is mine. A just state protects my property; it does not redistribute it."', 'Libertarian think-tank pamphlet, 2020 (adapted)', 'Identify the theory of justice in the source and explain it.', 'Libertarianism/Nozick\'s entitlement theory: holdings acquired through just acquisition and voluntary transfer are just, and the state should protect property rather than redistribute.')],
    evaluate=[('Rawls\'s theory provides the most convincing account of justice.', ['fair procedure (veil of ignorance)', 'protects the least advantaged', 'combines liberty and equality'],
               ['Nozick: ignores rights to property and history', 'utilitarians: welfare maximisation', 'Sen: focus on capabilities and real lives', 'cosmopolitan critics: limited to nation-states'],
               'Rawls offers a powerful framework balancing liberty and equality, but it is contested and needs extending to global and capability-based concerns.'),
              ('Justice requires the redistribution of wealth from rich to poor countries.', ['Singer\'s duty to aid', 'historical injustice (colonialism)', 'global inequality extreme'],
               ['Nozick: no duty beyond respecting rights', 'aid effectiveness concerns (Moyo)', 'state sovereignty and national responsibility'],
               'There are strong moral arguments for global redistribution, especially to remedy historical injustice, but disagreement over duties and effectiveness limits consensus.')],
    extended=[('Apply two theories of justice to a contemporary global issue and evaluate which offers the more convincing analysis.', 'E.g. climate finance, refugees, vaccine distribution, global taxation; apply Rawls, utilitarianism, Nozick or Sen; compare implications and weaknesses.', 8, 'long')],
))

R.facts('ch09-st3', F(
    terms=[('minority rights', 'rights that protect members of national, ethnic, religious or linguistic minorities, including the right to enjoy their culture, religion and language (ICCPR Art. 27)'),
           ('UNDRIP', 'the UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples (2007), which recognises Indigenous peoples\' rights to self-determination, land and free, prior and informed consent'),
           ('free, prior and informed consent', 'the principle that Indigenous peoples must be consulted and give consent, without coercion and with full information, before projects affecting their lands proceed'),
           ('Yogyakarta Principles', 'a 2006 set of principles, drafted by human rights experts, on applying international human rights law to sexual orientation and gender identity'),
           ('intersectionality', 'the idea, developed by Kimberlé Crenshaw, that forms of discrimination such as race, gender and class overlap and compound each other'),
           ('gender equality', 'equal rights, responsibilities and opportunities for people of all genders')],
    facts=[('Which of the following four states voted against UNDRIP in 2007 but later endorsed it?', 'Australia, Canada, New Zealand and the United States', ['Brazil, China, India and Russia', 'France, Germany, Italy and Spain', 'Japan, South Korea, Mexico and Chile'], 'All four have large Indigenous populations and settler-colonial histories.'),
           ('Which of the following Indian Supreme Court judgments decriminalised consensual same-sex relations in 2018?', 'Navtej Singh Johar v Union of India', ['Puttaswamy v Union of India', 'Kesavananda Bharati v State of Kerala', 'Vishaka v State of Rajasthan'], 'The court read down Section 377 of the Penal Code.'),
           ('Which of the following movements emerged in Iran after the death of Mahsa (Jina) Amini in police custody in 2022?', '"Woman, Life, Freedom"', ['#MeToo', 'Ni Una Menos', 'Me Too India'], 'Protests challenged compulsory hijab rules and the regime.'),
           ('Which of the following ICCPR articles protects the rights of ethnic, religious and linguistic minorities?', 'Article 27', ['Article 1', 'Article 6', 'Article 19'], 'It protects the right to enjoy culture, religion and language in community with others.')],
    why=[('Explain why the rights of Indigenous peoples are often in conflict with development projects.', 'Mining, dams, oil and agribusiness frequently target Indigenous lands rich in resources; states and companies prioritise national growth and revenue, while Indigenous communities claim collective land rights and FPIC (e.g. Standing Rock, Belo Monte dam).'),
         ('Explain why LGBTQ+ rights remain highly contested globally.', 'Some states see them as part of universal non-discrimination (Yogyakarta Principles, UN independent expert since 2016), while others frame them as contrary to religion or national culture; dozens of states still criminalise same-sex relations.'),
         ('Explain why women\'s rights can be described as both universal and contested.', 'CEDAW is ratified by nearly all states, but reservations, customary and religious laws, and political backlash (e.g. Taliban restrictions, abortion debates) mean the meaning and priority of women\'s rights remain contested.')],
    contrast=[('minority rights', 'Indigenous rights', 'Minority rights protect groups that are numerically smaller within a state (ethnic, religious, linguistic); Indigenous rights protect peoples with historical continuity with pre-colonial societies and emphasise collective land rights and self-determination (UNDRIP).'),
              ('formal equality', 'substantive equality', 'Formal equality means equal treatment in law; substantive equality aims for equal outcomes in practice, which may require special measures such as quotas or affirmative action.')],
    misc=[('The UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples is a binding treaty that Indigenous peoples can enforce in international courts.', 'UNDRIP is a non-binding General Assembly declaration; some of its provisions reflect binding law (e.g. ILO Convention 169, regional court rulings), and some states have incorporated it domestically (e.g. Canada\'s 2021 UNDRIP Act).'),
          ('Minority rights mean minorities can ignore national laws.', 'Minority rights protect culture, religion and language within the framework of human rights and national law; they do not exempt minorities from general laws.')],
    cases=[('Standing Rock protests (2016–2017)', 'The Standing Rock Sioux and allies protested against the Dakota Access Pipeline near their water source and sacred sites; the pipeline was completed in 2017.', 'Indigenous rights versus development', 'the case shows conflict over free, prior and informed consent and the power imbalance between Indigenous communities, the state and energy companies'),
           ('the Rohingya in Myanmar', 'The Rohingya were largely stripped of citizenship by Myanmar\'s 1982 Citizenship Law and faced military "clearance operations" in 2017; The Gambia brought a genocide case at the ICJ in 2019.', 'the rights of minorities', 'denial of citizenship made a minority especially vulnerable, and international legal action has tried to provide accountability'),
           ('Iran\'s "Woman, Life, Freedom" movement', 'After Mahsa Amini died in morality police custody in 2022, mass protests led by women challenged compulsory hijab laws; hundreds of protesters were killed and several executed.', 'women\'s rights as contested rights', 'the movement claimed universal rights against a state that justifies restrictions on religious grounds'),
           ('Same-sex marriage in Taiwan (2019)', 'After a 2017 Constitutional Court ruling, Taiwan became the first place in Asia to legalise same-sex marriage, despite a 2018 referendum against it.', 'LGBTQ+ rights and the role of courts', 'a court interpreted constitutional equality to extend rights in the face of majority opposition')],
    apply=[('A mining company plans an open-pit mine on land claimed by an Indigenous community. Explain two rights the community could invoke and one argument the government might make.', 'Rights: collective land rights and FPIC (UNDRIP Arts. 26 and 32; ILO 169); cultural rights (ICCPR Art. 27); right to a healthy environment. Government: national development, jobs and revenue; state ownership of subsoil resources. 2+2 marks.', 4),
           ('Using the concept of intersectionality, explain why Indigenous women may face particular rights violations.', 'They may face combined discrimination based on gender, ethnicity and poverty – e.g. high rates of violence (Canada\'s inquiry into missing and murdered Indigenous women and girls, 2019) and poor access to services – that neither gender-based nor ethnicity-based analysis alone captures.', 3)],
    sources=[('"Consultation is not consent. They came, they explained the dam, and then they built it."', 'Statement by an Indigenous leader in the Brazilian Amazon, 2015 (adapted)', 'With reference to the source, explain the principle of free, prior and informed consent.', 'FPIC requires that Indigenous peoples can say no, not just be informed; the source criticises a process where consultation occurred but consent was not obtained (e.g. Belo Monte dam).'),
             ('"Our laws reflect our faith and our families. Foreign governments that tie aid to so-called sexual rights are practising a new colonialism."', 'Statement by a parliamentarian supporting anti-LGBTQ legislation, 2023 (adapted)', 'Identify the argument in the source and explain one counter-argument.', 'Cultural relativist/sovereignty argument that LGBTQ+ rights are foreign impositions; counter: non-discrimination is part of universal rights (ICCPR Art. 26, Toonen) and LGBTQ+ people exist within every culture.')],
    evaluate=[('The rights of Indigenous peoples are now well protected by international law.', ['UNDRIP 2007, ILO 169', 'regional court rulings (Awas Tingni, Saramaka)', 'Canada\'s UNDRIP Act'],
               ['non-binding declaration', 'land conflicts and violence against defenders', 'FPIC often ignored'],
               'International law recognises Indigenous rights more than ever, but protection on the ground remains weak.'),
              ('The rights of LGBTQ+ people will eventually be accepted as universal human rights.', ['trend of decriminalisation (India 2018, Botswana 2019)', 'courts and UN mandates'],
               ['backlash (Uganda 2023, Russia "LGBT movement" declared extremist 2023)', 'religious opposition'],
               'There is a clear long-term trend towards recognition, but strong backlash makes universal acceptance uncertain.')],
    extended=[('Analyse why the rights of one group you have studied remain contested, and evaluate the effectiveness of efforts to protect them.', 'Choose e.g. Rohingya, Uyghurs, Indigenous peoples in Brazil, LGBTQ+ people in Uganda, Afghan women; identify sources of contestation (sovereignty, culture, economics) and evaluate protection mechanisms.', 8, 'long')],
))

R.facts('ch09-st4', F(
    terms=[('politicization of rights', 'the use of human rights claims for political or strategic advantage, rather than consistent principles'),
           ('selectivity', 'applying human rights criticism or action to some states but not others, depending on interests or alliances'),
           ('targeted sanctions', 'restrictive measures such as asset freezes and travel bans directed at specific individuals or entities rather than whole economies'),
           ('Magnitsky Act', 'a type of law, first passed in the US in 2012, allowing sanctions on individuals responsible for serious human rights abuses or corruption'),
           ('whataboutism', 'responding to criticism by pointing to the critic\'s own failings rather than addressing the charge')],
    facts=[('Which of the following laws was named after a Russian lawyer who died in pre-trial detention in 2009?', 'The Magnitsky Act', ['The Leahy Law', 'The Patriot Act', 'The Helms-Burton Act'], 'Sergei Magnitsky had exposed a tax fraud.'),
           ('Which of the following did the EU adopt in December 2020 to sanction human rights abusers worldwide?', 'The EU Global Human Rights Sanctions Regime', ['The EU Digital Services Act', 'The Schengen Borders Code', 'The Dublin Regulation'], 'It is often called the "EU Magnitsky Act".'),
           ('Which of the following states was suspended from the UN Human Rights Council in April 2022?', 'Russia', ['China', 'Saudi Arabia', 'Venezuela'], 'The General Assembly voted 93 to 24 with 58 abstentions.'),
           ('Which of the following countries was the target of a 2022 diplomatic boycott of the Winter Olympics by the US and some allies over human rights?', 'China', ['Qatar', 'Russia', 'Saudi Arabia'], 'Officials stayed away from the Beijing Games over Xinjiang.')],
    why=[('Explain why human rights criticism is often accused of being selective.', 'Powerful states tend to criticise adversaries (e.g. Iran, Russia) while muting criticism of allies or trade partners (e.g. Saudi Arabia, Egypt), which suggests strategic interests shape human rights diplomacy.'),
         ('Explain why sanctions on whole economies can harm human rights.', 'Broad sanctions can reduce access to food, medicine and jobs for ordinary people while elites adapt (e.g. Iraq in the 1990s), so they may violate socio-economic rights even when aimed at rights violators.'),
         ('Explain why states with poor human rights records seek membership of the Human Rights Council.', 'Membership allows them to influence resolutions, block scrutiny of themselves and allies, and gain legitimacy; this has led to criticism of the Council\'s credibility.')],
    contrast=[('targeted sanctions', 'comprehensive sanctions', 'Targeted sanctions freeze assets or ban travel of specific individuals or entities responsible for abuses; comprehensive sanctions restrict trade with an entire country, affecting the whole population.'),
              ('principled human rights diplomacy', 'politicised human rights diplomacy', 'Principled diplomacy applies the same standards to all states, including allies; politicised diplomacy uses rights selectively to pressure rivals while protecting partners.')],
    misc=[('Because human rights are sometimes used politically, they have no value.', 'Selective use reveals hypocrisy but does not invalidate the norms themselves; victims and NGOs use them regardless of states\' motives.'),
          ('Targeted sanctions never affect ordinary people.', 'Targeted sanctions on major firms or banks can still have wider economic effects, and over-compliance by banks can block humanitarian trade.')],
    cases=[('Western responses to the killing of Jamal Khashoggi (2018)', 'US intelligence assessed that Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman approved the operation, but the US did not sanction him directly, citing strategic ties with Saudi Arabia.', 'selectivity in human rights diplomacy', 'strategic and economic interests limited the response, illustrating the politicization of rights'),
           ('sanctions over Xinjiang', 'In 2021 the EU, UK, US and Canada sanctioned Chinese officials over Uyghur detention; China retaliated by sanctioning MEPs and academics, and the EU–China investment agreement was frozen.', 'rights in foreign policy', 'human rights were used as a foreign policy tool, provoking counter-sanctions and showing the costs of rights diplomacy'),
           ('the 2022 FIFA World Cup in Qatar', 'Rights groups highlighted migrant worker deaths and the kafala system; Qatar introduced some labour reforms but rejected Western criticism as hypocritical.', 'the politics of rights criticism', 'scrutiny produced some reforms but also accusations of Western double standards'),
           ('Russia\'s suspension from the Human Rights Council (2022)', 'After reports of killings in Bucha, Ukraine, the General Assembly suspended Russia; many Global South states abstained, criticising selectivity.', 'the politicization of human rights institutions', 'the vote showed rights being used against a violator, but abstentions revealed concerns about double standards')],
    apply=[('A state imposes sanctions on a rival for human rights abuses but continues arms sales to an ally accused of similar abuses. Explain two consequences of this inconsistency.', 'It undermines the credibility of the sanctioning state\'s human rights claims; it allows the target to dismiss criticism as political (whataboutism); it weakens the universal norm. 2 marks each.', 4),
           ('Identify whether each is a targeted or comprehensive sanction: (a) an asset freeze on a police chief, (b) a ban on all imports from a country, (c) a travel ban on a minister.', '(a) targeted; (b) comprehensive; (c) targeted. 1 mark each.', 3)],
    sources=[('"When the West sanctions our officials it calls this human rights. When its allies bomb hospitals it calls this complicated."', 'Spokesperson of a foreign ministry under Western sanctions, 2024 (adapted)', 'With reference to the source, explain the charge of selectivity in human rights diplomacy.', 'The speaker argues that Western states apply human rights sanctions to rivals but excuse allies\' violations, so rights are used as political tools rather than consistent principles.'),
             ('"Targeted sanctions are our way of saying: you personally will not shop in Paris or bank in London while you torture people at home."', 'EU parliamentarian, 2021 (adapted)', 'Explain, with reference to the source, the purpose of targeted human rights sanctions.', 'They impose personal costs (travel bans, asset freezes) on individuals responsible for abuses, stigmatising them while avoiding harm to the general population.')],
    evaluate=[('Human rights are used mainly as a tool of foreign policy by powerful states.', ['selective sanctions', 'muted criticism of allies (Saudi Arabia)', 'Iraq 2003 justified partly by rights'],
               ['NGOs and victims use rights independently', 'states sometimes accept costs for rights (Xinjiang sanctions)', 'Global South states use rights too (South Africa at ICJ)'],
               'Powerful states often instrumentalise rights, but rights also have independent force through law, NGOs and victims, so they are not merely tools.'),
              ('Sanctions are an effective way to improve human rights.', ['South Africa apartheid sanctions', 'targeted sanctions stigmatise abusers'],
               ['Iran, North Korea, Cuba unchanged', 'humanitarian harm (Iraq 1990s)', 'rally-round-the-flag effects'],
               'Sanctions rarely change regimes\' behaviour alone; they are most effective when multilateral, targeted and combined with diplomacy and domestic pressure.')],
    extended=[('Evaluate the claim that the selective use of human rights in foreign policy undermines their legitimacy.', 'Discuss cases (Khashoggi, Xinjiang, Qatar, Russia suspension, Gaza responses); distinguish norm legitimacy from state credibility; judge.', 8, 'long')],
))

P1['ch09'] = dict(
    src=[('"There is no culture in which torture is a value. Those who invoke tradition to defend repression are usually defending their own power."',
          'Speech by a UN High Commissioner for Human Rights, 2019 (adapted)',
          'Identify three claims made in {S}.',
          ['no culture values torture', 'tradition is invoked to defend repression', 'those who do so are usually defending their own power'],
          'Explain, with reference to {S}, a universalist response to cultural relativism.',
          'Universalists argue that core rights (e.g. freedom from torture) are shared across cultures, and that relativist claims are often used by elites to justify repression rather than reflecting genuine cultural values.'),
         ('"Every society balances the individual and the community differently. We do not ask Europe to adopt our family structures; we ask Europe not to impose its own."',
          'Address by an Asian government minister, 2018 (adapted)',
          'Identify three points in the argument made in {S}.',
          ['societies balance individual and community differently', 'his state does not ask Europe to adopt its family structures', 'Europe should not impose its own'],
          'Explain, with reference to {S}, the argument of cultural relativism.',
          'Values and rights are shaped by culture (e.g. the balance between individual and community); outsiders should respect these differences rather than impose their own standards.'),
         ('"Rawls asked us to imagine choosing rules without knowing our place in society. Imagine choosing the rules of the global economy without knowing your passport."',
          'Essay on global justice, 2021 (adapted)',
          'Identify three ideas in {S}.',
          ['Rawls\'s veil of ignorance', 'choosing rules without knowing one\'s place in society', 'applying this to the global economy without knowing one\'s nationality'],
          'Explain, with reference to {S}, how theories of justice can be applied to global inequality.',
          'Extending the veil of ignorance globally suggests people would choose rules protecting the least advantaged worldwide (e.g. fair trade, aid), supporting cosmopolitan justice.'),
         ('"We sanctioned the generals in Myanmar but not the princes in the Gulf. Our values seem to depend on who sells us oil."',
          'Opinion column in a European newspaper, 2022 (adapted)',
          'Identify three points made in {S}.',
          ['Myanmar\'s generals were sanctioned', 'Gulf rulers were not', 'values seem to depend on economic interests such as oil'],
          'Explain, with reference to {S}, the concept of the politicization of human rights.',
          'Human rights responses vary according to strategic and economic interests, so rights are applied selectively, which undermines credibility.')],
    cmp=[(0, 1, 'whether human rights should be universal', ['both discuss the relationship between culture and rights', 'both reference the role of outsiders and power'],
          ['Source A is universalist; Source B is relativist', 'A sees tradition as a pretext for repression; B sees cultural difference as legitimate']),
         (2, 3, 'the role of justice and values in global politics', ['both suggest the current global system is not fully just', 'both criticise the influence of power or position'],
          ['Source C offers a philosophical framework (Rawls); Source D criticises state practice', 'C concerns economic distribution; D concerns human rights diplomacy'])],
    ev=[(0, 1, 'human rights are universal and should not vary across cultures.', ['Source A: no culture values torture', 'own knowledge: Vienna 1993, local movements against FGM'],
         ['Source B: different balance of individual and community', 'own knowledge: Bangkok Declaration, weak relativism']),
        (1, 3, 'the promotion of human rights by Western states lacks legitimacy.', ['Source D: selectivity', 'Source B: imposition', 'own knowledge: Iraq 2003, Khashoggi'],
         ['norms have independent value', 'own knowledge: Global South and local actors also promote rights'])],
)

# =============================================================================================
# ch10 Development and Sustainability – contested meanings of development
# =============================================================================================
R.facts('ch10-st1', F(
    terms=[('development', 'a multidimensional process of improving people\'s well-being, including economic, social, political and environmental dimensions'),
           ('economic growth', 'an increase in the real output of goods and services in an economy, usually measured by GDP'),
           ('human development', 'the expansion of people\'s freedoms and opportunities to live long, healthy and creative lives (UNDP)'),
           ('capability approach', 'Amartya Sen\'s view that development should be judged by the real freedoms (capabilities) people have to be and do what they value'),
           ('functionings', 'in Sen\'s approach, the beings and doings a person achieves, such as being well-nourished or taking part in community life'),
           ('Buen vivir', 'an Andean concept of "living well" in harmony with community and nature, included in the constitutions of Ecuador (2008) and Bolivia (2009)')],
    facts=[('Which of the following economists wrote Development as Freedom (1999)?', 'Amartya Sen', ['Jeffrey Sachs', 'Walt Rostow', 'Dambisa Moyo'], 'Sen won the 1998 Nobel Prize in Economics.'),
           ('Which of the following Pakistani economist, with Sen, created the Human Development Index in 1990?', 'Mahbub ul Haq', ['Muhammad Yunus', 'Raúl Prebisch', 'Ha-Joon Chang'], 'The first Human Development Report was published by UNDP in 1990.'),
           ('Which of the following countries replaced GDP growth as its main goal with "Gross National Happiness"?', 'Bhutan', ['Nepal', 'Costa Rica', 'Norway'], 'The concept is in Bhutan\'s 2008 constitution.'),
           ('Which of the following did Martha Nussbaum contribute to the capability approach?', 'A list of ten central human capabilities', ['The Gini coefficient', 'The stages of growth model', 'The dependency theory of underdevelopment'], 'Nussbaum\'s list includes life, bodily health and practical reason.')],
    why=[('Explain why economic growth does not always lead to development.', 'Growth may be unequally distributed, environmentally destructive or concentrated in extractive sectors; it may not improve health, education or freedoms for most people (e.g. resource-rich states with high GDP but poor human development outcomes).'),
         ('Explain why Sen argued that famines are related to freedom.', 'Sen found that famines result from loss of entitlements (ability to obtain food) rather than only food shortage, and that no substantial famine has occurred in a functioning democracy with a free press, because governments are held accountable.'),
         ('Explain why development is described as a contested concept.', 'Actors disagree on its goals (growth, well-being, freedom, sustainability), its measurement and how to achieve it; Western, Indigenous and post-development perspectives define it differently.')],
    contrast=[('economic growth', 'economic development', 'Growth is a quantitative increase in output (GDP); development is a broader qualitative improvement in living standards, capabilities, equity and institutions.'),
              ('capabilities', 'functionings', 'Capabilities are the real freedoms or opportunities a person has to achieve valued states; functionings are the states and activities actually achieved – e.g. the ability to eat well vs actually being well-nourished.')],
    misc=[('A rising GDP per capita means that everyone in a country is better off.', 'GDP per capita is an average; growth may be concentrated among the rich, and it ignores non-market activities, inequality and environmental costs.'),
          ('Sen\'s capability approach ignores income.', 'Sen sees income as an important means to capabilities, but argues it is not an end in itself; the same income may yield different capabilities depending on health, gender or disability.')],
    cases=[('Equatorial Guinea', 'Oil revenues have given Equatorial Guinea one of the highest GDP per capita levels in sub-Saharan Africa, but a large share of the population lives in poverty and health outcomes are poor.', 'the gap between growth and development', 'high average income has not translated into broad human development, because oil wealth is concentrated'),
           ('Kerala, India', 'Kerala has achieved high literacy, life expectancy and low infant mortality comparable to some richer countries despite modest income per capita.', 'human development without high income', 'public investment in health and education expanded capabilities, supporting Sen\'s argument'),
           ('Bhutan\'s Gross National Happiness', 'Bhutan measures progress using nine domains, including psychological well-being, culture, governance and ecology, and its constitution requires 60% forest cover.', 'alternative definitions of development', 'Bhutan defines development beyond GDP, emphasising well-being and sustainability'),
           ('Ecuador\'s constitution and the rights of nature', 'Ecuador\'s 2008 constitution recognises rights of nature (Pachamama) and buen vivir; in 2023 voters chose to halt oil drilling in part of Yasuní National Park.', 'Indigenous and post-development perspectives', 'development is redefined to include harmony with nature rather than only growth')],
    apply=[('Country A has higher GDP per capita than Country B, but lower life expectancy and literacy. Explain which is more developed using the capability approach.', 'Country B – people have greater real freedoms (to live long and be educated); Sen\'s approach judges development by capabilities, not income alone. Mention distribution and conversion factors.', 3),
           ('A government plans a large dam that will raise GDP but displace 50,000 farmers. Outline two perspectives on whether this is development.', 'Growth/modernisation view: energy and growth benefit the nation; capability/human rights view: displacement reduces the capabilities and rights of the affected; post-development/Indigenous view: imposes an external model. 2 marks each.', 4)],
    sources=[('"Development can be seen as a process of expanding the real freedoms that people enjoy."', 'Amartya Sen, Development as Freedom, 1999', 'With reference to the source, explain Sen\'s view of development.', 'Development is the expansion of real freedoms (capabilities) – to be healthy, educated, participate politically – rather than merely income growth; income is a means, not the end.'),
             ('"Our grandparents lived well without what you call development. Buen vivir is not about having more; it is about living in balance."', 'Kichwa community leader, Ecuador, 2019 (adapted)', 'Identify the view of development in the source and explain how it differs from growth-based approaches.', 'Buen vivir – well-being through balance with community and nature; unlike growth-based approaches it rejects accumulation and consumption as the main measure of progress.')],
    evaluate=[('Economic growth is the most important factor in achieving development.', ['growth funds health and education', 'China\'s poverty reduction', 'jobs and incomes'],
               ['growth without distribution (Equatorial Guinea)', 'Kerala shows public policy matters', 'environmental limits'],
               'Growth is usually necessary for sustained development but is not sufficient; distribution, institutions and sustainability determine whether it improves lives.'),
              ('The capability approach is the most useful way to understand development.', ['multidimensional', 'focus on people\'s real freedoms', 'basis of HDI'],
               ['hard to measure', 'individualist (critics from buen vivir perspective)', 'does not specify a list (Sen)'],
               'The capability approach is highly influential and humane, though measurement challenges and cultural critiques limit it as a complete framework.')],
    extended=[('Compare two contrasting definitions of development and evaluate their implications for policy in one country you have studied.', 'Compare growth-based and capability-based (or buen vivir) definitions; apply to e.g. India, Ecuador, Ethiopia; show how definitions lead to different priorities (GDP targets vs health/education vs environmental protection).', 8, 'long')],
))

R.facts('ch10-st2', F(
    terms=[('GDP per capita', 'the total value of goods and services produced in a country in a year divided by its population'),
           ('Human Development Index (HDI)', 'a UNDP composite index of life expectancy, education (mean and expected years of schooling) and gross national income per capita (PPP)'),
           ('Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI)', 'an index developed by OPHI and UNDP (2010) measuring overlapping deprivations in health, education and living standards across ten indicators'),
           ('Gini coefficient', 'a measure of income inequality from 0 (perfect equality) to 1 or 100 (one person has all income)'),
           ('Happy Planet Index', 'an index created by the New Economics Foundation (2006) combining well-being and life expectancy relative to ecological footprint'),
           ('purchasing power parity (PPP)', 'a method of adjusting income for differences in the cost of living between countries')],
    facts=[('Which of the following are the three dimensions of the Human Development Index?', 'Health, education and standard of living', ['Income, inequality and happiness', 'Democracy, freedom and corruption', 'Health, environment and security'], 'Measured by life expectancy, schooling and GNI per capita (PPP).'),
           ('Which of the following describes a Gini coefficient of 0?', 'Perfect income equality', ['Perfect inequality', 'Zero economic growth', 'No people in poverty'], 'Higher values indicate more inequality; South Africa has one of the highest Gini coefficients.'),
           ('Under the global MPI, a person is considered multidimensionally poor if deprived in at least what share of weighted indicators?', 'One third', ['One tenth', 'One half', 'Two thirds'], 'The ten indicators are grouped into health, education and living standards.'),
           ('Which of the following countries has frequently ranked at or near the top of the Happy Planet Index?', 'Costa Rica', ['Qatar', 'The United States', 'Luxembourg'], 'Costa Rica combines high well-being and life expectancy with a modest ecological footprint.')],
    why=[('Explain why the HDI is considered a better measure of development than GDP per capita alone.', 'It combines income with health and education, reflecting the capability approach; countries with similar income may differ widely in life expectancy and schooling, which GDP alone hides.'),
         ('Explain why the Gini coefficient is useful alongside average income measures.', 'Averages can hide large inequalities; the Gini shows how income is distributed, so two countries with the same GDP per capita may have very different living conditions for the majority.'),
         ('Explain why the MPI can reveal poverty that income measures miss.', 'It measures deprivations directly (nutrition, child mortality, schooling, sanitation, electricity, housing, assets), showing who lacks basic needs even when incomes are above a poverty line, and how deprivations overlap.')],
    contrast=[('GDP per capita', 'the HDI', 'GDP per capita measures average economic output per person; the HDI combines income with life expectancy and education to measure human development more broadly.'),
              ('income poverty', 'multidimensional poverty', 'Income poverty is having income below a threshold (e.g. the World Bank\'s $3.00 a day line from 2025); multidimensional poverty is experiencing several overlapping deprivations in health, education and living standards.')],
    misc=[('A country with a high HDI has no inequality.', 'The HDI uses averages; the inequality-adjusted HDI (IHDI) shows that inequality can reduce human development significantly, even in high-HDI countries such as the US.'),
          ('The Happy Planet Index measures only how happy people say they are.', 'It combines reported well-being and life expectancy with ecological footprint, so it measures sustainable well-being, not happiness alone.')],
    cases=[('India\'s reduction in multidimensional poverty', 'UNDP and OPHI reported that around 415 million people in India exited multidimensional poverty between 2005/06 and 2019/21.', 'the use of the MPI', 'the MPI showed improvements in sanitation, cooking fuel and schooling that income data alone would not capture'),
           ('South Africa\'s inequality', 'South Africa has one of the highest Gini coefficients in the world (about 0.63) despite being an upper-middle-income country.', 'the importance of measuring inequality', 'average income disguises extreme inequality rooted in apartheid, which affects development outcomes'),
           ('the United States and the inequality-adjusted HDI', 'The US ranks high on HDI but falls significantly when the index is adjusted for inequality.', 'limitations of average measures', 'high average achievement can coexist with unequal access to health and education'),
           ('Costa Rica', 'Costa Rica abolished its army in 1948, invests heavily in health and education and generates almost all its electricity from renewables.', 'alternative measures of development', 'it performs well on the Happy Planet Index despite modest GDP, showing well-being can be achieved sustainably')],
    apply=[('Country X has GDP per capita of $12,000 and a Gini coefficient of 0.60; Country Y has GDP per capita of $9,000 and a Gini of 0.30. Explain which country\'s poorest citizens are likely to be better off and why.', 'Country Y\'s poorest are likely better off: although average income is lower, income is much more equally distributed, so the bottom groups receive a larger share; in X high inequality means many receive far less than the average.', 3),
           ('Identify the most appropriate measure for each question: (a) Are people living long, educated lives? (b) How equally is income shared? (c) How many households lack electricity, sanitation and schooling at the same time?', '(a) HDI; (b) Gini coefficient; (c) MPI. 1 mark each.', 3)],
    sources=[('"GDP measures everything, in short, except that which makes life worthwhile."', 'Robert F. Kennedy, speech at the University of Kansas, 1968', 'With reference to the source, explain one limitation of GDP as a measure of development.', 'GDP measures market output but not health, education, happiness, community or environmental quality; it can rise due to harmful activity, so it is a poor measure of well-being.'),
             ('"The national average says our region is middle income. But in our villages, half the children lack clean water and one in three is stunted."', 'District health officer, 2022 (adapted)', 'Explain, with reference to the source, why multidimensional measures are important.', 'Averages conceal local deprivation; the MPI and similar measures capture overlapping deprivations (water, nutrition) at household level, helping target policy.')],
    evaluate=[('The HDI is the best available measure of development.', ['multidimensional', 'widely used and comparable', 'based on capability approach'],
               ['ignores inequality (unless IHDI), environment, freedom', 'averages hide variation', 'weights are arbitrary'],
               'The HDI is a major improvement on GDP, but a combination of measures (IHDI, MPI, Gini, environmental indicators) gives a fuller picture.'),
              ('Measures of development reflect the values of those who create them.', ['GDP reflects market priorities', 'HPI reflects environmental values', 'GNH reflects Bhutanese culture'],
               ['indicators like life expectancy are widely valued', 'technical comparability'],
               'All measures embed choices about what matters; recognising this helps explain debates over development, though some core indicators enjoy broad consensus.')],
    extended=[('Using at least three measures, analyse the level of development of one country you have studied.', 'Use GDP per capita, HDI/IHDI, MPI, Gini, HPI for a specific country (e.g. Brazil, Nigeria, Qatar); explain what each reveals and their limits; reach a judgement.', 8, 'long')],
))

R.facts('ch10-st3', F(
    terms=[('modernisation theory', 'the theory that all societies can develop by passing through similar stages, from traditional to modern, often with Western help and investment (Rostow)'),
           ('dependency theory', 'the theory that underdevelopment in the periphery results from its exploitative integration into a global economy dominated by the core'),
           ('world-systems theory', 'Immanuel Wallerstein\'s theory that the capitalist world economy is divided into core, semi-periphery and periphery, with surplus flowing to the core'),
           ('semi-periphery', 'in world-systems theory, states with a mix of core and peripheral characteristics, such as Brazil, India or South Africa'),
           ('Prebisch–Singer hypothesis', 'the argument that the prices of primary commodities tend to decline relative to manufactured goods over time, disadvantaging commodity exporters'),
           ('import substitution industrialisation (ISI)', 'a strategy of replacing imports with domestic production behind tariff protection, used in Latin America from the 1950s')],
    facts=[('Which of the following wrote The Stages of Economic Growth: A Non-Communist Manifesto (1960)?', 'Walt Rostow', ['Andre Gunder Frank', 'Immanuel Wallerstein', 'Raúl Prebisch'], 'Rostow proposed five stages from traditional society to high mass consumption.'),
           ('Which of the following is the final stage of Rostow\'s model?', 'The age of high mass consumption', ['Take-off', 'Drive to maturity', 'Preconditions for take-off'], 'The stages are traditional society, preconditions, take-off, drive to maturity and high mass consumption.'),
           ('Which of the following economists coined the phrase "the development of underdevelopment"?', 'Andre Gunder Frank', ['Walt Rostow', 'Amartya Sen', 'Jeffrey Sachs'], 'Frank argued that integration into the capitalist system underdeveloped Latin America.'),
           ('Which of the following divides the world economy into core, semi-periphery and periphery?', 'Immanuel Wallerstein\'s world-systems theory', ['Rostow\'s stages of growth', 'The Washington Consensus', 'Keynesian economics'], 'Wallerstein developed the theory from the 1970s.')],
    why=[('Explain why dependency theorists argue that trade with developed countries can keep poorer countries underdeveloped.', 'Peripheral countries export low-value primary commodities and import high-value manufactures; declining terms of trade (Prebisch–Singer), foreign ownership and profit repatriation transfer surplus to the core, limiting industrialisation.'),
         ('Explain why modernisation theory was influential during the Cold War.', 'It offered a non-communist route to development, promising that Western aid, investment and values would lead newly independent states to prosperity, countering the appeal of Soviet-style socialism.'),
         ('Explain why the rise of East Asian economies challenged dependency theory.', 'South Korea, Taiwan and later China integrated into the world economy through export-led industrialisation and moved from periphery towards core, suggesting that integration need not perpetuate underdevelopment.')],
    contrast=[('modernisation theory', 'dependency theory', 'Modernisation theory sees underdevelopment as an early stage overcome by adopting modern institutions, investment and integration; dependency theory sees underdevelopment as caused by exploitative integration into the capitalist world economy.'),
              ('dependency theory', 'world-systems theory', 'Dependency theory focuses on a two-part relationship between core and periphery and often recommends delinking; world-systems theory analyses the whole capitalist world economy, adds a semi-periphery and allows mobility between zones.')],
    misc=[('Rostow\'s model shows that every country will inevitably reach high mass consumption.', 'Rostow\'s stages are a generalisation based on Western history; critics argue it ignores colonial legacies, external constraints and different paths, and many countries have not followed it.'),
          ('Dependency theory argues that poor countries are poor because of their culture.', 'Dependency theory rejects cultural explanations; it attributes underdevelopment to structural exploitation in the global economy.')],
    cases=[('South Korea\'s development', 'South Korea moved from one of the poorest countries in the 1950s to a high-income economy through state-led, export-oriented industrialisation and investment in education.', 'challenges to dependency theory', 'a peripheral economy integrated into global markets and moved to the core, contrary to dependency predictions'),
           ('Zambia\'s dependence on copper', 'Copper accounts for the majority of Zambia\'s export earnings; falls in copper prices have contributed to fiscal crises and a debt default in 2020.', 'dependency and commodity reliance', 'reliance on a primary commodity exposes Zambia to price volatility and limits diversification, as dependency theorists warn'),
           ('Latin American import substitution industrialisation', 'From the 1950s Brazil, Argentina and Mexico used tariffs and state enterprises to industrialise; the strategy faltered with the 1980s debt crisis.', 'policy responses inspired by dependency theory', 'ISI aimed to reduce dependence on core manufactures, but inefficiency and debt limited its success'),
           ('China as a semi-peripheral power', 'China moved from exporting low-cost manufactures to leading in electric vehicles, solar panels and batteries, while still having large poorer rural regions.', 'world-systems mobility', 'China illustrates movement from periphery towards core, with semi-peripheral features')],
    apply=[('Classify each country in world-systems terms and justify: (a) Germany, (b) Brazil, (c) Malawi.', '(a) core – high-value manufacturing, capital exporter; (b) semi-periphery – industrial base but commodity exports and inequality; (c) periphery – agricultural exports (tobacco), low wages, aid dependence. 1 mark each.', 3),
           ('A country exports cocoa beans and imports chocolate. Using the Prebisch–Singer hypothesis, explain a likely long-term problem.', 'Prices of primary commodities (cocoa) tend to fall relative to manufactured goods, so the country must export more to import the same amount (worsening terms of trade); most value-added is captured abroad. Côte d\'Ivoire and Ghana grow most cocoa but capture a small share of chocolate revenue.', 3)],
    sources=[('"We grow the coffee, but the money is made in the roasting houses of Hamburg and Seattle."', 'Ethiopian coffee cooperative leader, 2018 (adapted)', 'With reference to the source, explain a dependency theory perspective on global trade.', 'The periphery exports raw commodities while the core captures value-added processing and branding; surplus flows to core economies, perpetuating underdevelopment.'),
             ('"Fifty years ago this country was poorer than Ghana. Today it builds the phones and ships the world buys."', 'Article on South Korea\'s economic history, 2020 (adapted)', 'Explain, with reference to the source, why South Korea is used to challenge dependency theory.', 'South Korea moved from periphery to core through industrialisation and exports, showing integration into the global economy can produce development rather than permanent dependence.')],
    evaluate=[('Dependency theory remains relevant to understanding development today.', ['commodity dependence (Zambia, DRC)', 'value chains capture value in the core', 'debt flows'],
               ['East Asian success', 'China and India\'s rise', 'domestic governance matters'],
               'Dependency theory remains useful for understanding structural constraints on commodity exporters, but it underestimates the agency of states that have industrialised.'),
              ('Modernisation theory is outdated.', ['Eurocentric and linear', 'ignores colonialism and structural constraints', 'environmental limits to mass consumption'],
               ['many states still pursue growth and industrialisation', 'investment and education matter', 'East Asian take-offs'],
               'Its linear and Eurocentric assumptions are outdated, but some elements – investment, education, institutions – remain central to development policy.')],
    extended=[('Compare how modernisation theory and world-systems theory would explain the development of one country you have studied.', 'Apply both to e.g. China, Nigeria, Bangladesh or Brazil: stages, investment and values vs core–periphery position and surplus flows; evaluate which explains more.', 8, 'long')],
))

